  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "Sorting by Rules",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "This lesson introduces how choosing features affects the way data is sorted. Taking on the role of data scientists, students sort monster cards using yes/no questions, identify each other’s rules, and solve secret sorting challenges. Through hands-on activities and discussion, students explore how different features produce different classifications and why more data may be needed to identify a rule.",
        "preparation": "- Print and cut one set of monster cards per groups of 2–4 students \n- Print the activity guide: one per student",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you take on the role of a data scientist to learn how to use information to sort data. You will practice by sorting monsters and finding secret sorting rules."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Reading a Decision Tree",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students take on the role of data scientists as they learn to read and trace decision trees. They explore how training data can lead to mistakes, act out a human decision tree, and suggest questions to improve its predictions. Finally, they use Sketch Lab to trace a recycling robot’s decisions and identify where its tree needs a fix.",
        "preparation": "- Print the Human Decision Tree Cards (one set for each group: three question cards and five animal data cards).\n- Put each group's animal data cards in order, with the platypus card last.\n- Read the Human Decision Tree: Teacher Guide and plan a clear space for each group.",
        "student_overview": "How does a decision tree reach an answer? You will follow items through a decision tree, one question at a time, to see how it makes a prediction. Then you'll check the tree's predictions against what each item really is."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Who Writes the Rules?",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students act as Data Scientists and find out who writes the rules inside a computer system. Pairs first write keyword rules to block spam and keep patching them as new emails get through or get caught by mistake. Then pairs explore a simulated game chat and notice which messages it blocks or hides. Groups sort the seven gaming chat rules from the chat into human-written and computer-learned rules, using what they saw in the game as evidence. Students ultimately learn that both human-written and computer-learned rules exist within systems.",
        "student_overview": "You will act as a Data Scientist and find out who writes the rules inside a computer system. First, you will write rules to block spam from an email inbox. Then you will explore a game chat and notice which messages it blocks or hides based on rules. By the end of the lesson, you will be able to answer: who writes the rules?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Learning from Examples",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students are Data Scientists for a wildlife park that wants to predict when elephants will visit a waterhole. They help Dr. Osei, the park's data scientist, turn camera photos into records. A record is all the data about one photo, such as the season, the time of day and whether elephants came. Students then test four models trained on different records. They explain why a model may predict poorly for a case its records left out. They also learn about PAWS, a real model trained on ranger patrol records.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Data Makes AI - Lesson 4 - Activity Guide for each student.",
        "student_overview": "Today you are a Data Scientist on Dr. Osei's team at a wildlife park. The park wants a model that predicts when elephants will visit the waterhole. Your job is to choose the records the model needs. Then see what happens when some records are missing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Curating Your Own Dataset",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do people’s choices about a dataset shape what a model can predict?**\n\nStudents act as data scientists, choosing which data to use to train a model. Using the Smart Room Inspector, they decide what makes a room clean or messy, choose features, and sketch decision trees. A surprise room challenges their predictions and shows why the data people collect matters.",
        "preparation": "- print one activity guide per pair",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you’ll use an app that predicts if a bedroom is **Clean Enough** or a **Total Disaster**. You’ll choose the features that help the most and turn numbers into categories. Then you’ll draw a decision tree that sorts rooms. Last, you’ll test your tree on a surprise room. Can it predict the right label?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Balanced vs. Unbalanced Data",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can two copies of the same app give different answers?**\n\nIn this lesson, students work as data scientists and audit Playlist Bot, an app that picks songs for the school dance. In Song Piles, groups line up song cards by release year and lay a pencil at a cutoff year. Students see that every Banger sits on one side, and that the person who chose the cutoff shaped what counts as a good dance song. In AI Studio, pairs test the school's bot with a new hit, Soda Pop, and find the question that led to its prediction. They sort and filter a second bot's records to check balance, then test it with Soda Pop. The same app gives different answers because each bot was trained on different records. Pairs record their audit on a Model Card, and each student writes who the school's bot might leave out.",
        "preparation": "- Print the Song Piles Cards handout (one set of 10 song cards for each group) and cut out the cards.\n- Print the Lesson 6 activity guide (one for each pair).\n- Print the Model Card handout (one for each pair).\n- Give each group a pencil to mark the cutoff.",
        "student_overview": "How can two copies of the same app give different answers? You'll check Playlist Bot, an app that picks songs for the school dance. You'll sort its song records. Then you'll test two versions of the bot with the same new song. Last, you'll find out who its records leave out."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "How to Train Your Decision Tree",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students take on the role of data scientists working with the previsualization team from the Amazon Studios career tour. This team decides which movie scenes get a 3D rough draft before filming. After meeting the team in a short video, students explore a dataset of past scenes labeled Rough Draft or Just Film It. They train various decision trees on the same scenes using different features, and compare what makes a scene risky to film. They finish by naming an assumption the data makes about real movies.",
        "student_overview": "You’ll work as a data scientist helping a movie team decide which scenes need a rough draft before filming. You’ll train decision trees using different features and find out which ones help the model make good choices. Then your team will each add a new feature and test your trees on scenes they’ve never seen. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Testing and Tracing Predictions",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students learn that a model's mistakes are not random. Every prediction a decision tree makes can be traced back to the training data that produced it. Working as data scientists for a movie studio's marketing team, students build the skill of tracing: reasoning backward from a wrong prediction to the specific examples that taught it. Through tracing, they discover that mistakes come from the data in different ways. Sometimes the data doesn't have enough of the right examples, and sometimes it never recorded the information that mattered.\n",
        "student_overview": "When an AI model makes a mistake, where does it come from? In this lesson, you'll learn to trace a model's wrong answer back to the data that taught it. You'll find out that different problems in the data cause different kinds of mistakes, and that some can be fixed by changing the data while others can't."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "Combining Features",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "This lesson, students learn that improving a model isn’t always about more data. Sometimes the better move is asking a better question. Working with the virtual production team from the Amazon Studios career tour, students combine two or more features into one new feature using Either or Both, test it by hand on scene cards, then check whether AI Studio agrees. They discover that a well-built combined feature can make a model simpler and more accurate, while a combination of unrelated features does nothing or makes things worse. Students finish by completing a model card that names what their model does well and whose scenes it might get wrong.",
        "preparation": "* Print and cut one set of 8 double-sided scene cards per pair. The scene and features are described on the front while the proper label is printed on the back.\n* Print one copy of the decision tree per pair. \n",
        "student_overview": "Can a better question make a smarter model? In this lesson, you’ll invent new features by combining old ones, test them by hand and in AI Studio, and find out which combinations actually help. Then you’ll write a model card that explains what your model is good at and who it might get wrong."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Accuracy ≠ Fairness",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students learn that a model can be accurate and still be unfair. Working as investigators, they move from one person's complaint about an app, to experiencing the problem firsthand, to finding the pattern of people the app keeps failing, and then trace that pattern back to a decision made while building the model. Across three cases, students see that unfairness can start in different places: in who was included in the data, in which features the model uses, or in how the right answer was defined. By the end, students understand that a high accuracy score only shows that a model matches its labels, not that its data or labels were fair to everyone.",
        "student_overview": " If an app works for most people, is that good enough? In this lesson, you'll investigate apps that work well for most users but keep failing a group of people. You'll use the app yourself, dig into the data behind it, and figure out the choice that caused the problem."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Tracing Bias to Outcome",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students ask who is responsible when an AI system gives people unfair results. After a short video with Dan Rosen, who works in responsible AI, they learn the Ethics Trace: five steps that follow an unfair result back to the data choices and the people behind it. Pairs try it on a real-world case. Along the way, students see that harm can happen even when no one means it, and that people can reasonably disagree about who is to blame. They finish by making a comic in Sketch Lab about AI bias.",
        "preparation": "- Print one activity guide per student.\n- Print the case cards: one card per pair. Cut on the dashed lines.\n- Have sticky notes available for the Gallery Walk activity\n- Post signs A-D in the four corners of the room (optional).\n- Teacher reference: the case card key has model answers and a link to each real case.",
        "student_overview": "When an AI system gives someone an unfair result, who is responsible? Today you’ll trace unfair results back to the data choices people made using a real-world case. Then you’ll make a comic that tells a story about AI bias."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Data Makes AI Project (Day One)",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students begin the Data Makes AI Project as Data Scientists. They choose one of five datasets, record its label and features in the project guide, and plan their model on a Project Map before they train it. Then they train Tree 1 in AI Studio, test it for a user they choose, and add the tree to their map to compare it with their own rule.",
        "preparation": "- Print one Data Makes AI Project guide for each student. Students use it in Lessons 12–15.\n- Preview the five datasets in Lesson 12, Level 1 and the Project Map in Level 2.",
        "student_overview": "Today you start the Data Makes AI Project. You pick a dataset, choose what your model will predict and who it is for, and train your first decision tree. Then you test it for your user."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Data Makes AI Project (Day Two)",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students look for a problem in their data. They train Tree 1 again, find the test records it gets wrong, and look for a category with very few records. They pick a data move (FEATURE, ADD, CUT or BALANCE) from the Data Moves Reference, train Tree 2 on the Fixed dataset, and compare the two trees on their Project Map.",
        "preparation": "- Print one Data Moves Reference for each student. Students keep it for the rest of the project.\n- Students need their Data Makes AI Project guide.",
        "student_overview": "Did changing the data help your model? Today you find what your tree gets wrong, pick a way to fix the data, and train Tree 2 to see what changed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-14",
      "name": "Data Makes AI Project (Day Three)",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students test their model for people who might use it. They read the User Profile cards, choose two users, make a prediction for each in AI Studio, and trace each user's path on Tree 2 in their Project Map. Then they complete an Ethics Trace about who could be affected by the model's mistakes and write a draft model card.",
        "preparation": "- Print the User Profile cards for each dataset: one set of four cards per student or group.\n- Students need their Data Makes AI Project guide.",
        "student_overview": "Does your model work for different users? Today you test it for two people, trace their predictions, and decide when your model should and should not be used."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-14",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-15",
      "name": "Data Makes AI Project (Day Four)",
      "absolute_position": 15,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Students test their model for a new user. Then classmates visit each other's Project Maps, test each model and leave sticky notes. Students use the notes to revise their model card and reflect on what a data scientist controls when they build a model.",
        "preparation": "- Have a small stack of sticky notes for each student or group.\n- Students need their Data Makes AI Project guide and their User Profile cards.",
        "student_overview": "Today classmates test your model, and you test theirs. You use what they find to finish your model card."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-15",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-16",
      "name": "Vocabulary Flashcards",
      "absolute_position": 16,
