    {
      "key": "11b0f76a-e63c-40ff-a2f4-baf0efa4c1f5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "name": "Vocabulary Flashcards"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson_activity.key": "11b0f76a-e63c-40ff-a2f4-baf0efa4c1f5",
        "lesson.key": "lesson-16",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "6-12-aid-data-makes-ai-2026"
      }
    }
  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "b47944dc-680f-4b2d-9ac6-c9bee2b2a854",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see that people sort the same things in different ways, based on the feature(s) they pay attention to. AI systems also use features that they recognize in order to make classifications.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the four pictures. Give students 30 seconds to pick the one that does not belong.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Look at the four pictures. Which one does not belong? Why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Every item can be the one that doesn't belong. It depends on what you pay attention to. Take several answers from students and push for the because each time. Try to emphasize what students might have been using to classify the objects: \"So you were paying attention to ___.\" A main takeaway is that everyone looked at the same four pictures and sorted them differently because they each chose something different to pay attention to.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Why might an AI system have misclassified this dog as a muffin?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Some students will say the AI is dumb or broken. That's fine for a first impression. To encourage more critical thinking, ask the students, \"What do the dog and the muffin have in common?\" Students will likely name the tan color, the round shape, and the three dark spots. Then ask, \"What would help you tell them apart?\" Students might say fur, ears, or a paper wrapper. Point out that some details trick you because both things have them, while other details help you sort correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b47944dc-680f-4b2d-9ac6-c9bee2b2a854",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6a5ac79-bd10-420e-8ec9-f59dabcc6510"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "822a9037-8bc8-41c3-8a73-10f7cb5b0c67",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students sort monster cards with yes/no questions. They see that the person sorting chooses the feature.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of 12 monster cards. Give each student a copy of the Sorting by Rules activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of 2–4.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give groups a few minutes to explore the monster cards. The students should record their observations in the Round One section of their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask for volunteers to share some of their observations. Write the answers on the board. Label the list \"features.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define feature.\n\n**Say**: Everything on our list is a feature. A feature is a characteristic you can observe about something. It can be something you can see, like a color, or it could be a quality that is harder to observe, like softness or temperature.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define data.\n\n**Say**: Our monster cards are data. Data is information that has been collected and recorded.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Each group creates one secret yes/no question and sorts its monsters into a Yes group and a No group based on this rule. They should record this rule in the Round Two section of their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If a group picks a question with more than two answers (\"What color is it?\"), help them make it yes/no (\"Is it green?\").\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have two groups swap and attempt to guess each other's question. Students should record what they believe the rule to be in their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How did you figure out the other group's sorting rule?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for something like \"We looked at what all the Yes monsters had in common.\" If a group guessed a different rule that still works for every card, make a big deal of it. Ask, \"Both rules work. What new monster would show us which rule is the real one?\" Point out that the same cards can be sorted lots of ways, and the people sorting get to decide how.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define classification.\n\n**Say**: You put each monster in a category based on one or more of its features. That is called classification.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Groups pick one of their two groups and sort it again with a second yes/no question. Students complete the Round Three section of their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Draw one group's sort on the board as yes/no branches. Define branching logic.\n\n**Say**: Each answer sends you a different way. That is branching logic. What you just built by hand is called a decision tree. People build them, and computers can build them too.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Why might it be useful to add a second yes/no question?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students explaining that one question still leaves things mixed together. \"It makes smaller groups\" is a start, but push for more by asking \"Why would you want smaller groups?\" A stronger answer names what they wanted to separate, like \"so we can tell the spiky ones apart from the fuzzy ones.\" Students should name a real purpose behind their further sorting.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Monster Sorter"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "822a9037-8bc8-41c3-8a73-10f7cb5b0c67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8c208385-bde6-4d91-a8bf-020ae56c55f2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f504f7b8-64d4-407f-b527-a2ec9938a02a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: People who build sorting systems like these as their job are called data scientists. A big part of their job is what you just did at your tables: figuring out which features matter for sorting something into the right category. For instance, a data scientist at a music streaming company asks the same question about songs: which features help predict whether a listener will skip a song? Is it the tempo? The genre? The length? Not every feature turns out to matter.\n\nIn this unit, you become the data scientists.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What do you think is the hardest part of that job? Why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for answers that connect to what students did today: picking which detail matters, a question that didn't sort the way they wanted, or monsters that were hard to place. When you hear one, ask \"What was hard about choosing your question today?\" to bring them back to their own experience.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Career Spotlight: Data Scientist"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f504f7b8-64d4-407f-b527-a2ec9938a02a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8c208385-bde6-4d91-a8bf-020ae56c55f2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3a3aa2c-dafa-4b84-840e-ba62ce839502",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students name features they could sort by, then determine the sorting rule used in the levels. They see that with too little data, a new item could fit more than one box, and that more data helps them find the real rule.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students in pairs\n\n**Say**: Each of these levels has a rule that decides what goes in each box. Your job is to determine what that rule is.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1:\n* **Level 1**: Students look at items in green, blue and red boxes and pick how they are sorted (by color).\n* **Level 2**: Students predict where a pink circle with a black border goes. Box One holds circles and Box Two holds a pink square, so it could go in either box. The best answer is \"We need more training data to know.\"\n* **Level 3**: Students click Run, then drag each item into a box. The new rule is outline color: Box One black, Box Two red, Box Three blue.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Verify students are progressing through levels and help any students that are struggling\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Find the Secret Rule",
        "progression_name": "Find the Secret Rule"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3a3aa2c-dafa-4b84-840e-ba62ce839502",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8c208385-bde6-4d91-a8bf-020ae56c55f2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88e7638f-acbc-45e6-80d9-1d64a7531449",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 4. Pairs pick one level. Partner A sorts the items on screen with a secret rule. Partner B acts as the computer and guesses how Partner A sorted.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that Partner B doesn't watch while Partner A sorts. If Partner B guesses a different rule that also fits, ask the pair what new item would tell the two rules apart.\n",
        "progression_name": "Your Secret Rule"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88e7638f-acbc-45e6-80d9-1d64a7531449",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8c208385-bde6-4d91-a8bf-020ae56c55f2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67e904b8-98df-4481-9f40-f618beaf9595",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What is one question about your own life that could be answered by a sorting system?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Good examples sort things into groups, like \"Will I skip this song?\", \"Do I need a jacket?\", or \"Is this text spam?\" Some students will offer a question that doesn't sort anything, like \"What's for dinner?\" Help them reshape it by asking \"What would the two piles be?\" Strong answers also mention what the system would look at to decide, like the weather or who sent the text. Students don't have to share anything personal.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students record their responses in the Wrap Up section of the activity guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67e904b8-98df-4481-9f40-f618beaf9595",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8c208385-bde6-4d91-a8bf-020ae56c55f2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de4da438-802a-45c9-938e-fbfe67f3798f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Levels 5–6:\n\n* **Level 5**: Students flip vocabulary flash cards for feature, data, classification and branching logic. They can shuffle the cards or start from the definition.\n* **Level 6**: Students drag each word to its definition, then click Submit.\n",
        "progression_name": "Lesson Vocabulary"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de4da438-802a-45c9-938e-fbfe67f3798f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0929c4fc-646a-4812-aadf-609696f9febe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04a3e8b0-a521-4369-926a-77dacf6feeba",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<!-- Apply as a Teaching Tip callout in Level Builder -->",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students will mention details, features, or yes/no questions. Others will say something like \"it just knows.\" Both are normal today, so don't correct anyone. You'll come back to this question later in the unit, and these answers show where students are starting."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04a3e8b0-a521-4369-926a-77dacf6feeba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0929c4fc-646a-4812-aadf-609696f9febe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a024cd5-a61f-4292-8254-0c1610fdb11f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a024cd5-a61f-4292-8254-0c1610fdb11f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0929c4fc-646a-4812-aadf-609696f9febe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4fe180b-269c-4197-bdad-7c58cfe956d9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the pictures. Ask students if there are any images that appear labeled incorrectly."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4fe180b-269c-4197-bdad-7c58cfe956d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "331ea484-28ee-4eca-969c-a70aefd58342"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c3c0c6f-0f52-4358-a1d7-83591f0b659a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Researchers built this AI system to see if people would trust it. It sorts pictures into Husky or Wolf. Look at picture C. It's a husky, but the system said Wolf. Next, we'll look at the pictures the system was trained on.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The system focused on the snow in the background, not the animal. Every wolf picture it was trained on had snow in it. Let students find this on the next slide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c3c0c6f-0f52-4358-a1d7-83591f0b659a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "331ea484-28ee-4eca-969c-a70aefd58342"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "38b27072-2d28-48bd-b2f3-6ace421530f3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What features do you think the system focused on to sort a wolf from a husky?\n\nTurn and Talk: Give partners two minutes, then take two or three answers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students compare what the system used with what a person would use. Listen for the snow in every wolf picture, then features like ears, eyes or fur. If students only talk about the animals, ask, \"Is there anything outside of the wolf itself that is the same in every wolf picture?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "38b27072-2d28-48bd-b2f3-6ace421530f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "331ea484-28ee-4eca-969c-a70aefd58342"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6ea5591-15b5-4ffb-bb7a-917de79ca265",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The system focused on the snow, not the animal. Every wolf picture it was trained on had snow in it, so it predicts Wolf when it sees snow.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In the real study, 27 graduate students who had taken a machine learning class saw the system's results. Before they saw what it focused on, 10 of them trusted it. After they saw it used the snow, only three did."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6ea5591-15b5-4ffb-bb7a-917de79ca265",
        "lesson_activity.key": "331ea484-28ee-4eca-969c-a70aefd58342"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1500f773-7b92-4242-a80b-cdabf6415c8d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1500f773-7b92-4242-a80b-cdabf6415c8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "331ea484-28ee-4eca-969c-a70aefd58342"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74e42715-2f31-4b0f-ae66-57d91d4f874e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that the questions from Lesson 1 form a decision tree, which reaches a prediction one question at a time.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Meet the Decision Tree"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74e42715-2f31-4b0f-ae66-57d91d4f874e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "764f85b0-b1f5-4d1c-a649-d4e5cc7b4e25",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In Lesson 1, you sorted monsters by asking yes/no questions. Here are two of those questions, placed in order. Each answer creates a path, to another question or to the end.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "764f85b0-b1f5-4d1c-a649-d4e5cc7b4e25",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ed5dcc0-b92b-467b-89e2-18361c4c2027",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which features do monsters of the same type share?\n\nTurn and Talk: Give partners one minute, then take two or three answers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should compare the monsters in each type and name features they all share. If a student names another feature, ask, \"Does every monster in that type have it?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ed5dcc0-b92b-467b-89e2-18361c4c2027",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d16836d-74f2-40b0-9095-03b74f07c04e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define decision tree: a series of questions about features. Each answer leads to another question or a prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define label: the category that a system predicts based on its features. Connect it to the monster type.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to trace the monster's path.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define prediction: the best guess based on the data available."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d16836d-74f2-40b0-9095-03b74f07c04e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "483a753d-e975-42c0-86ac-56ddb2df4c28",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We all make predictions every day. You see dark clouds and predict it will rain. You hear the bell and predict lunch is next. You smell popcorn and predict someone is watching a movie. Each time, you use what you notice to make your best guess.\nAI systems make predictions too. A decision tree asks questions about features, one at a time. The prediction is the label it reaches based on those features, like the monster's type on the last slide.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "483a753d-e975-42c0-86ac-56ddb2df4c28",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdf7c9e3-f60f-4fac-9a9a-764f72c88785",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that a decision tree can only use its questions to classify, so an animal whose questions don't fit will get a wrong prediction.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Human Decision Tree"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdf7c9e3-f60f-4fac-9a9a-764f72c88785",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e892b06c-8494-4b3a-a7be-bc1e1f1a7c91",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We just traced our first decision tree. Now we're going to create one in the classroom. Some of you will be Askers. Each Asker holds a question card and asks its question. Everyone else is a Traveler. Each Traveler holds an animal data card and answers from it. Let's see how it works.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e892b06c-8494-4b3a-a7be-bc1e1f1a7c91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dadb8aee-9eb9-4748-aa49-2617ce29f17e",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of four to ten. Give each group a clear space.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group three question cards and five animal data cards. Keep the animal data cards face down in a stack, with the platypus last.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Play the Human Decision Tree video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dadb8aee-9eb9-4748-aa49-2617ce29f17e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "155b7be9-2283-4107-bcc0-965266e019cc",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group pick three Askers. Each Asker takes one question card and stands in the spot shown on it. Everyone else is a Traveler. Go over the three lines on the slide. Travelers line up by the stack of animal data cards. One at a time, each takes the top card and walks to Question One.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that Askers stand a few steps apart, in the order on their cards. Ask an Asker, \"What do you ask, and where do you send each answer?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The platypus card is last on purpose. The tree predicts Reptile, but a platypus is a mammal: its card says Makes Milk: Yes."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "155b7be9-2283-4107-bcc0-965266e019cc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cee5b0e8-18da-4a95-af28-22a9158f0d60",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Leave this slide up while groups play.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Bring the class together after every group has walked the platypus card. Display the facts."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cee5b0e8-18da-4a95-af28-22a9158f0d60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b739250f-5cc3-45d7-8b7f-5fd45a9d9956",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Our tree called the platypus a Reptile. Read these facts about platypuses. What kind of animal is a platypus?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b739250f-5cc3-45d7-8b7f-5fd45a9d9956",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2802347-228c-4fa6-9bac-239963076f83",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which question put the platypus on the wrong path?\n\nTurn and Talk: Give them one minute.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students trace the platypus's path to find where the tree first went wrong. Listen for \"Does it lay eggs?\" If groups blame \"Does it have fins?\", ask, \"Where did the platypus first go a different way than the dog?\"",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Fix the Tree"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2802347-228c-4fa6-9bac-239963076f83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eecff242-1f3c-4c0b-b303-b00f3123e7e5",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here's the tree with one new question. Now the platypus says Yes to \"Does it make milk?\" and reaches Mammal. The tree only uses the questions it was given. People chose these questions, so people can fix them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eecff242-1f3c-4c0b-b303-b00f3123e7e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55108c54-14fb-4da1-a742-1ac874b817bc",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that tracing each item's path shows which question led to a wrong prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1: Students trace three items through the Recycling Robot tree and move each one next to the bin it predicts. Then they drag the wrench to the spot where a new question would fix the wrong prediction.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Recycling Robot Trace",
        "progression_name": "Recycling Robot Trace"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55108c54-14fb-4da1-a742-1ac874b817bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6a99310-aae3-4a93-9713-9b01d75586a9",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who name a bin without following the questions. Ask, \"Which questions did the water bottle answer on its way to Trash?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how to save their sketch in Level 1.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If a student can't find their sketch, have them open their Downloads folder and look for the newest file.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how to upload: click Add/Remove Attachments, then Upload File, and choose the sketch from the Downloads folder.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 2: Students upload their sketch and answer one question: what question they would add to help the robot sort better.",
        "progression_name": "Recycling Robot Trace"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6a99310-aae3-4a93-9713-9b01d75586a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50e32087-eca9-492d-85f3-af6c3758e9eb",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that each student uploaded a sketch before answering. Look for answers that name the new question but not where it goes. Ask, \"Which answer leads to your new question?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' Level 2 answers to check whether they can compare a prediction with an item's label and propose a question that fixes a wrong prediction."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50e32087-eca9-492d-85f3-af6c3758e9eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20ce9651-7300-43a2-a305-c2f3c5c028d4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cffabd3b-c03e-48be-8a73-1ce842469b01",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 3: Students open the End of Lesson Survey link and answer each question. While they work, complete the Teacher End of Lesson Survey linked on the same level."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cffabd3b-c03e-48be-8a73-1ce842469b01",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aee70778-6c24-4d28-8f28-1edbfef69918"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48e0df53-e67c-4c31-8e40-6467775038b6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48e0df53-e67c-4c31-8e40-6467775038b6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aee70778-6c24-4d28-8f28-1edbfef69918"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af491b0b-9ee0-40e2-bce2-e9f962e439a8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students compare following a recipe with learning from experience. This sets up the two kinds of rules they'll analyze inside computer systems today.\n\n**Say**: These two people both make great pasta, but they learned how in different ways.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Are these two chefs doing the same thing? Why or why not?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Both chefs make good pasta, but they got there differently. One follows steps someone else wrote, and the other learned from experience. Many students will stop at \"one uses a book.\" Push them to say how the chef knows what to do without one. If someone says \"she practiced,\" ask what she'd do with an ingredient she's never cooked before. That question brings out the difference: the recipe cook can only follow the steps, while the chef can adjust.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students vote with their fingers: 1 for Recipe Cook, 2 for Chef, or 3 for Both.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This vote is a prediction, not a quiz, so don't reveal an answer. Most students will pick the recipe cook, because \"computers follow instructions\" is what they already believe. Ask one or two students to defend their vote. If anyone picks Both, ask what part of a computer might be like the chef. Leave the answer open, since the lesson comes back to this vote at the end.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af491b0b-9ee0-40e2-bce2-e9f962e439a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97212326-a3de-4790-8a84-cbbc7650bca8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39879ee5-cee8-4dd8-9ad1-d002d44cc4bb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student/group a copy of the Who Writes the Rules? Activity guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39879ee5-cee8-4dd8-9ad1-d002d44cc4bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2915a4d6-bb15-4838-86ad-0aff8f41a49a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1530018-e322-4a71-8cc2-da2d0f438d72",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students write human-written rules for blocking spam, then test them on new emails until the rules start to fail. Running into those limits is what makes computer-learned rules make sense later.\n\n**Say**: Your job is to keep junk mail out of the inbox. You'll write rules, and the computer will follow them exactly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students in pairs to work through the day's activities.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How do we know this is spam? What gives it away?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students will quickly spot the clues: \"WINNER!!\", \"FREE,\" a huge prize, a deadline, and a sender name they don't recognize. Push them past spotting clues and toward writing rules a computer could check exactly. If a student says \"it sounds fake,\" ask what exact words or patterns the computer should look for. \"Sounds fake\" is a judgment, not a rule a computer can follow, and noticing that gap sets up the rest of Spam Patrol.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use this email as the guided practice for the activity and model writing two different rules in the My Rulebook section of the Activity Guide:\n\n* If the email has \"WINNER\" Junk\n* If the email has \"FREE\" Junk\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Groups test the email against their rules and add a new rule if it gets through.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Spam Patrol",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "\"Fr3e\" gets past a rule that blocks \"FREE.\" That's the point of this email: spammers change their spelling to sneak around exact rules."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1530018-e322-4a71-8cc2-da2d0f438d72",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2915a4d6-bb15-4838-86ad-0aff8f41a49a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c52218b-4e78-40d8-8981-60d8303c22e5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Groups test the email and add a rule for suspicious links if it gets through.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Groups test the email. If their rules block it, they add an exception.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "A \"WINNER\" rule blocks the teacher's real email. Every new rule can block good mail too, so the list of rules and exceptions keeps growing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c52218b-4e78-40d8-8981-60d8303c22e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2915a4d6-bb15-4838-86ad-0aff8f41a49a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02c5286a-181c-4c1d-9dda-04cc6175be03",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How many rules would you need to add to completely protect your email from spam?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should realize the rule list never ends. Spammers change their spelling, and every new rule risks blocking good mail. Some will say \"just add more rules.\" Point back to \"Fr3e\" and ask what happens tomorrow when a spammer types it a new way. If a student suggests the computer could figure out spam on its own, ask what it would need to look at to do that.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define algorithm\n\n**Say:** In the activity that we just did, if you combined all of the email rules together, you have created an algorithm that might teach an email program how to act and respond to incoming mail."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02c5286a-181c-4c1d-9dda-04cc6175be03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2915a4d6-bb15-4838-86ad-0aff8f41a49a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d18a9768-b7e9-4693-99c4-ea6d2f03250e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students explore a game chat system as players, watching how different inputs lead to different outputs. The patterns they notice here become their evidence in the next activity, when they open up the system and sort its rules.\n\n**Say**: You just joined a fictional gaming chat. The chat has a system that decides which messages get through and which ones don't. Your job is to figure out how it decides. The message you send is the input. What the chat does with it is the output.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 2:\n* **Level 1:** Students explore a simulated gaming chat app. They should infer what rules are being used to block chat messages.\n* **Level 2:** Students sort rules into 'human-written' or 'computer-learning' categories.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For Level 1, listen in when pairs reach a “Stop and think” panel. Pairs who click Next without talking should answer the question out loud first. In Set 5, point out the Report button on Blaze77’s message if no one has used it. For Level 2, ask pairs to point to what happened in the gaming chat that made them choose a side for a rule. Pairs who place a rule without reading its card should open it again and read the example out loud first. If a pair is stuck on Rule #7 (Mute) or disagrees about it, let them leave it where it is and bring the disagreement to the debrief. When a pair finishes, check that they add any missing rules to their Activity Guide.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Gaming Chat Algorithm",
        "progression_name": "Gaming Chat Algorithm"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d18a9768-b7e9-4693-99c4-ea6d2f03250e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2915a4d6-bb15-4838-86ad-0aff8f41a49a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "193e1295-dd4d-4042-84b6-903582c9a989",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define human-written rule\n\n**Say**: The spam rules you wrote today were human-written rules.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define computer-learned rule\n\n**Say**: Real spam filters learn from millions of emails that people mark as spam.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "193e1295-dd4d-4042-84b6-903582c9a989",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2915a4d6-bb15-4838-86ad-0aff8f41a49a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fc22457-1e3f-4577-bc52-d00507f2f682",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students see that one system can include both kinds of steps.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Who wrote most of the steps in this chat blocking algorithm?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Most of the rules are human-written, which surprises students who assumed the whole chat was AI. Count the cards together first, then shift from how many to which ones. The computer-learned rules both had to judge what a message means. Ask a group to explain why \"take out the trash\" got blocked but \"go away no one likes u\" got hidden. A strong answer links back to Spam Patrol: exact rules can't judge meaning, so that job went to the computer.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "So Who Writes the Rules?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fc22457-1e3f-4577-bc52-d00507f2f682",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2915a4d6-bb15-4838-86ad-0aff8f41a49a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd206fe8-6de1-4c4d-ba76-b800fe26a89b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 2 to answer the Check for Understanding free response question.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd206fe8-6de1-4c4d-ba76-b800fe26a89b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "858c93a2-ce86-41fe-a525-814f0f8e136a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c5ceea3-9387-4857-adc0-91a42cbe1e38",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "By now, students should be able to say \"both\" and back it up with an example from the gaming chat. Bring back the warm-up vote and ask who would change their answer. If a student just says \"both,\" ask for one rule that the chat follows and one rule that it learned. Students who can name a specific card for each side are ready to start on training data in the next lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c5ceea3-9387-4857-adc0-91a42cbe1e38",
        "lesson_activity.key": "858c93a2-ce86-41fe-a525-814f0f8e136a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84a0d5e0-cb6f-4a46-b4ee-808584b77e03",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84a0d5e0-cb6f-4a46-b4ee-808584b77e03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "858c93a2-ce86-41fe-a525-814f0f8e136a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "567d2038-c326-42bd-b281-64139acadd1d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "567d2038-c326-42bd-b281-64139acadd1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "858c93a2-ce86-41fe-a525-814f0f8e136a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "851a4efd-a8c3-47dd-8b2f-47eb5bef3870",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the picture and give students 30 seconds to think.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How would you teach a toddler what a firetruck is?\n* *Popcorn Share: take quick ideas from around the room. Write them on the board in two lists: \"Tell them rules\" and \"Show them pictures.\"*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students compare two ways to teach the same thing. Several answers are right. Listen for: rules (\"It has a ladder\") or showing (\"Point at one\"). Ask students what they paid attention to."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "851a4efd-a8c3-47dd-8b2f-47eb5bef3870",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8480759-26f4-4876-bc60-2bb1b4a66dcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "392e0b66-c862-4708-999b-5e05438b3bf7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Toddlers often learn by being shown things. Toddler A was shown these pictures. Toddler B was shown these pictures.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "392e0b66-c862-4708-999b-5e05438b3bf7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8480759-26f4-4876-bc60-2bb1b4a66dcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ac06858-d0d6-4c6c-b749-06977d85497f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts and the answers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**\n- *What would Toddler A call these, and why?*\n- *What would Toddler B call these, and why?*\n- *Turn and Talk: partners take one minute per question, then a few pairs share with the class.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice what each toddler's pictures have in common. Listen for: all of Toddler A's firetrucks were red. If students stall, ask, \"What color were all of Toddler A's firetrucks?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ac06858-d0d6-4c6c-b749-06977d85497f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8480759-26f4-4876-bc60-2bb1b4a66dcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "620adcd6-207c-4209-8cc8-0e77f52795ce",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that each photo becomes one record, with features they can see and a label they want to predict.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Learning from Examples activity guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Dr. Osei is a fictional character made up for this lesson. If students ask, tell them so."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "620adcd6-207c-4209-8cc8-0e77f52795ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c17b5cf-a1a9-4dc9-bc2a-9c628ba91a8b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you're Data Scientists on a team at a wildlife park. A data scientist uses data to answer questions. The park's data scientist, Dr. Osei, wants your help. You meet Dr. Osei, turn each camera photo into a record, and then explore Dr. Osei's records in AI Studio.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3:\n- **Level 1:** Students click each of the four questions and read Dr. Osei's answers, including what a camera trap is.\n- **Level 2:** Dr. Osei has photos from every camera in the park, and students record the data in three of them: they fill in the Record Card (Season, Time of Day, Water Level, Temperature and Elephants Visit) using the photo and its photo stamp, then click Save Record. They're done when Records (3 of 3) shows.\n- **Level 3:** Students explore Dr. Osei's Camera Trap Records: they switch between Table and Cards, try one Filter, and copy a record that matches one of their photos into the For the Record section of the activity guide.",
        "progression_name": "For the Record"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c17b5cf-a1a9-4dc9-bc2a-9c628ba91a8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0838309d-1614-4417-8c24-638aa4ac9fda",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who check each card against the photo. Ask: \"What in the photo tells you the Season?\" Look for students who compare the categories on a record card with their photo. Ask: \"How do you know this record matches your photo?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**\n\nWhat did you find in the Camera Trap Records?\n\nWhy does one line on every card look different?\n* *Turn and Talk: partners take one minute per question, then two or three pairs share with the class.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students name what they noticed while exploring, before the class names it together. Listen for: each card is one photo, Filter shows only some records, and Elephants Visit looks different. If students only say \"it's pink,\" ask, \"What does Elephants Visit tell Dr. Osei that the other lines don't?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Demo the Dataset tab while students watch. Open Lesson 4, Level 3 on the projector.\n- Click Table, then Cards, to show the same records two ways.\n- Click Filter, choose Season: Dry, and click Done. Point to the number of rows at the top.\n- Click Clear filter."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0838309d-1614-4417-8c24-638aa4ac9fda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "116716ac-41d6-4dfa-8727-2f42be4e91ca",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Each card is one record: all the data about one photo. In the table view, each record shows as one row. Filter shows only the records you pick, so you can look for patterns. The pink line on each card is the label: what the model predicts. The other lines are the features: what the model uses to predict. When you test a model, AI Studio shows the features in blue at the top. AI Studio numbers each card, like Row 1 here.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "116716ac-41d6-4dfa-8727-2f42be4e91ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95a04d04-3213-4692-bde6-b3fc6e471bea",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review feature and label.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students circle the label in the For the Record section of their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define record.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define training data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95a04d04-3213-4692-bde6-b3fc6e471bea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b49a0e3-6ee1-4830-bf5a-b9e0b96137bb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You have been working with records. A model is trained on a stack of records like the ones you filled in and explored in AI Studio. Training data is the set or collection of data used to train an AI model to recognize patterns or make predictions. Next, the job is to choose which records go in the stack.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b49a0e3-6ee1-4830-bf5a-b9e0b96137bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf22c0f4-a48e-4d4d-89e5-175e21078d7b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that a model can't answer a case its training records left out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the three cases and have students read them.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Which Data Works?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf22c0f4-a48e-4d4d-89e5-175e21078d7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53191621-616d-4b7f-9842-05e95d380ed0",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Dr. Osei's model has to work all year, day and night. It must get these three cases right. Each pretrained model you test was trained on different records. Enter the same three cases in each one. None of these models was trained on your photos. Each model has a data card that says who collected its records and when. Enter all three cases in each of the first three models. Check which cases each model gives an answer for, then read its data card. The tree asks one question at a time. Each answer sends the case down a branch until it reaches a prediction.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53191621-616d-4b7f-9842-05e95d380ed0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f40ff77c-65ca-4f11-81bf-6b5c3b3f24c1",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Demo the Testing tab while students watch. Open Lesson 4, Level 4 on the projector.\n- Point to Predict and based on at the top: the label is pink and the features are blue.\n- In the Make a prediction panel, set Season to Dry, Time of Day to Afternoon, Water Level to Low and Temperature to Hot. Click Predict.\n- Point to each step of the path in the AI predicts panel.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 4 to complete Levels 4 through 6:\n- **Level 4:** Students enter the three cases in Rangers by Day. Case 1 can't be entered, because its records were all collected during the day. Case 2 gives Yes. Case 3 gives No.\n- **Level 5:** Students enter the three cases in Dry Season Only. Case 1 gives Yes. Cases 2 and 3 can't be entered, because its records are all from the dry season.\n- **Level 6:** Students enter the three cases in Wet Season Cameras. Case 1 can't be entered, because its records are all from the wet season. Case 2 gives Yes. Case 3 gives No.",
        "progression_name": "Which Data Works"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f40ff77c-65ca-4f11-81bf-6b5c3b3f24c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a4ba04e-7336-4cda-bfbd-2a9f88a05e5d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who read the data card after a case fails. Ask: \"What does this data card say about when the records were collected?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**\n\nWhich case could each model not answer?\n\nWhy did each model miss a different case?\n* *Turn and Talk: partners talk for two minutes, then one pair per model shares.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students tie each failure to what that model's records left out. Listen for: no night records, no wet-season records, no dry-season records. If students say \"the model is bad,\" ask, \"What was missing from its records?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a4ba04e-7336-4cda-bfbd-2a9f88a05e5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2f702f7-dbe5-412b-9768-da27b81b8b6a",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 7 to complete Levels 7 through 8:\n- **Level 7:** Students enter the three cases in All Cameras. Case 1 gives Yes, case 2 gives Yes and case 3 gives No, so all three are right. Students fill in the All Cameras row in the What Each Model Predicts table.\n- **Level 8:** Students read a new data card, Visitor Photos, and answer on their own in a few sentences. This is assessment evidence: strong answers name what the records leave out (wet season, night).",
        "progression_name": "Filling the Gaps",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 8 answers show whether students explain a model's limits by its records. Look for answers that name the missing records: wet season and night."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2f702f7-dbe5-412b-9768-da27b81b8b6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29f52e9c-b9f3-482f-9910-096b4ef68986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e380c08-7c39-455d-bec7-54f5952b4c50",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-microphone\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Say:** PAWS is an AI that predicts where poachers may set snares in Queen Elizabeth National Park in Uganda. Poachers are people who hunt animals illegally, and snares are traps made of wire. PAWS was trained on years of ranger patrol records. Those records only show the places rangers walked, so a place with no records could still have snares. To test PAWS, rangers patrolled two areas they rarely visited that PAWS marked as risky, and they found many snares there.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e380c08-7c39-455d-bec7-54f5952b4c50",
        "lesson_activity.key": "51095042-461a-45ca-bf2a-d9aa533e16da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a211fbaf-020c-4ccd-b20e-8baac14bf5c9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a211fbaf-020c-4ccd-b20e-8baac14bf5c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "51095042-461a-45ca-bf2a-d9aa533e16da"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c84ffc3-e06a-4897-ac61-ae3b2ce0dff0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n* Imagine your parent or guardian walks into this room. Do they say it's clean enough or a total disaster?\n* What do you say?\n* Why might you disagree?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that people judge \"clean\" differently. Listen for the different things each person looks at first, like the floor or the bed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c84ffc3-e06a-4897-ac61-ae3b2ce0dff0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "85893d80-a092-4812-81bc-a1011a6b2377"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b18f0466-44f7-484f-9e91-5d6eb9ee075a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Revisit the vocabulary term label, as introduced in Lesson 2."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b18f0466-44f7-484f-9e91-5d6eb9ee075a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86f41883-613f-460a-8253-1f30dfeb9631",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we're going to look at the Smart Room Inspector app which tries to predict whether a room is clean or not. The answer it's trying to predict, Clean Enough or Total Disaster, is called the label.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86f41883-613f-460a-8253-1f30dfeb9631",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7475c4ca-1264-454c-aa1b-6a357891f4d1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that an app can predict whether a room is clean without showing what information it uses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to simulate taking photos of five rooms and see what the app predicts for each one.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Smart Room Inspector App",
        "progression_name": "Smart Room Inspector App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7475c4ca-1264-454c-aa1b-6a357891f4d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f1283c6-60c2-42bd-8eb6-ec14d3379a83",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who disagree with a prediction. Ask them what information in the photo the app might be using to make that prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n-  Do you agree with every prediction made by the app?\n- What information do you think the app is using to make its prediction?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students start to wonder what the app was trained on, because that question drives the rest of the lesson. Listen for guesses like clothes, dishes or the bed. Accept them, but don't confirm them yet."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f1283c6-60c2-42bd-8eb6-ec14d3379a83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc73ccba-9e8c-48db-9be4-53dfbdd446c6",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that when numbers are turned into categories, someone has to decide where each category starts and ends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that it is useful to transform numbers into categories to describe data more clearly, and give the example of temperature ranges and how they might be classified.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Numbers Into Categories",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students need more examples of turning numbers into groups, try one of these:\n* Phone battery: A battery percentage becomes a green, yellow, or red icon. Someone decided the icon turns red at 20%.\n* Clothing sizes: Measurements become S, M, or L. A medium at one store can be a large at another.\n* Hot sauce: Spiciness becomes Mild, Medium, or Hot. What counts as \"Hot\" depends on who's eating it.\n* Movie ratings: G, PG, PG-13, R\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc73ccba-9e8c-48db-9be4-53dfbdd446c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1537a01-152d-4c8f-879c-e81693ddc939",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "An app can't use \"it looks messy.\" It needs features it can measure, like how many items are on the floor. Right now those features are just numbers. Let's sort them into categories.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1537a01-152d-4c8f-879c-e81693ddc939",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c9a5a2e-507c-44bd-9551-70004a6082b9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 2 to make categories for \"Items on floor,\" to change where each category starts and ends, and to compare the Kid and Parent categories.",
        "progression_name": "Numbers Into Categories"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c9a5a2e-507c-44bd-9551-70004a6082b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94f08459-fff2-4928-b50c-8e094d49762b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch rooms near a boundary, like Room Four (7 items) and Room Seven (9 items). Ask pairs to move a cutoff by one or two and see which rooms switch categories. Encourage students to click Sort by items on floor to see a different view.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n\n- How did you decide where to put the line between your categories?\n- How might these categories affect the prediction made by the app?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that people decide where the lines between categories go, and those choices can change a prediction. Ask a few pairs with different cutoffs to share which room made them put the line there. Listen for: a room near a line could get a different prediction. If students say the categories don't matter because the numbers stay the same, ask, \"Which category is Room Seven (9 items) in for you? Would a rule like 'Lots of items means Total Disaster' change its prediction?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94f08459-fff2-4928-b50c-8e094d49762b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "279c6855-8308-45a0-a5f2-1cc91043ddce",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that some features sort the rooms better than others, and that a dataset only holds the features someone chose to collect.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 3.\n- **Level 3:** A Sketch Lab level in which students look at six photos and list features in the activity guide that would help judge whether a room is clean.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Curating a Dataset",
        "progression_name": "Identify Data Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "279c6855-8308-45a0-a5f2-1cc91043ddce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97b0287b-ad59-4c1a-87da-9f05b24f5158",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students who write \"messy\" or \"dirty\" as a feature. That's the label, not a feature. Ask, \"What makes it look messy?\" Also check how students handle Room Four. If a pair notices the laundry is clean, ask them which feature on their list would catch that.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 4.\n- **Level 4:** An AI Studio level in which students look at the dataset someone made from the Level 3 photos. They number the four features in the activity guide from most to least important.",
        "progression_name": "Curating a Dataset",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Read the written justifications in the activity guide. On target: students explain their feature choices in terms of what will help the tree sort rooms correctly (\"Dishes and bed status separate the clean rooms from the messy ones\"). Below target: students can't give a reason, or picked features without considering them (\"I just used the first two\")."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97b0287b-ad59-4c1a-87da-9f05b24f5158",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a86c3ec-3f1e-4b88-a9e3-e9fd674bfd91",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Turn and Talk. Have each pair turn to the pair next to them and compare their decision trees.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which feature(s) did you leave out of your decision tree, and why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that a dataset can only hold what someone chose to put in it. As you circulate, ask pairs to explain why a feature doesn't help, not just that it doesn't. If a pair kept Posters on Wall, let them make their case about clutter. Listen for comments about Room Four, like \"the floor number can't tell clean laundry from dirty laundry.\"",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a86c3ec-3f1e-4b88-a9e3-e9fd674bfd91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc9eae5a-d863-4a8c-9dae-ddd73d5b2e6f",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that a decision tree can only use what was measured.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the room image and its record. Give pairs one minute to trace the room through their tree. Ask for a show of hands for Clean Enough and for Total Disaster.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Tracing Your Tree",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Don't reveal the hidden items on the next slide until after the show of hands. The majority of students' decision trees should be able to accurately represent this entry as Clean Enough."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc9eae5a-d863-4a8c-9dae-ddd73d5b2e6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1ad7721-ec27-4d98-8795-cce07bef6e2d",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When the covers are pulled back, there is a pile of dirty dishes, cups and cans hidden on the bed!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1ad7721-ec27-4d98-8795-cce07bef6e2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "943f04aa-7ef6-4ca5-95e9-049785324f50",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What would someone need to change about the decision tree or the data to fix this?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students might first want to fix their tree. Point them back to the record, which says 0 dishes because whoever collected the data only counted what they could see. Nothing in the tree could have caught this. A strong answer will acknowledge that the data never included these dishes, and some students might suggest new ways to collect the data, like checking under the bed or the covers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "943f04aa-7ef6-4ca5-95e9-049785324f50",
        "lesson_activity.key": "615f729a-7ff8-4021-a16e-06d34e5c50c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66a1ef95-4fdc-4cfa-a227-d62bc00d0fb8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that what a dataset leaves out limits what the app can predict.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 5 to name a feature the dataset left out and explain what the Smart Room Inspector app loses because of it.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66a1ef95-4fdc-4cfa-a227-d62bc00d0fb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "add902a4-7b33-43db-82c8-2fd1780fb8cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92903c0a-9553-4059-878a-4130c0d29095",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "People curate a dataset by choosing which features to collect, how to sort numbers into categories, and how to label each record. Those choices shape what a model can predict.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "On target: students should be able to name a specific missing feature and connect it to a mistake the app could make (\"They didn't include what's under the bed, so the app would call a room clean even if it's hiding trash\").\n\nBelow target: students name a missing feature without explaining what the app loses, or say the app should \"just look harder.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92903c0a-9553-4059-878a-4130c0d29095",
        "lesson_activity.key": "add902a4-7b33-43db-82c8-2fd1780fb8cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89579942-95c6-4c29-85ed-0c9a5418d9b5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Ask a few students to answer it using an example from today, like the categories they made or the hidden dishes.\n\nListen for the idea that people choose which features to collect, where the lines between categories go, and which label each record gets, and that those choices decide what a model can use. If something was never measured, like dishes hidden under the covers, the model can't use it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89579942-95c6-4c29-85ed-0c9a5418d9b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "add902a4-7b33-43db-82c8-2fd1780fb8cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ad496c4-2974-4775-b97b-58fb8eb8ff6c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ad496c4-2974-4775-b97b-58fb8eb8ff6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "add902a4-7b33-43db-82c8-2fd1780fb8cb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6348092-7d89-48d8-bc93-00427bd1d432",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the slide and give students 30 seconds to think.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Your school's Playlist Bot is an AI. It was trained on songs that someone labeled Banger or Skip. The DJ plays Soda Pop, a new song that everyone loves. Playlist Bot says SKIP. Why might that happen?*\n* Turn and Talk: partners share one guess for 30 seconds, then take quick guesses from a few pairs. Don't confirm any yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should use what they know about training data to guess why an app would skip a song people love. Listen for: \"Maybe it was never trained on songs like this one\" or \"The person who labeled the songs didn't like this kind.\" If students say \"It's broken,\" ask: \"What was Playlist Bot trained on?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "The Bot Said Skip"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6348092-7d89-48d8-bc93-00427bd1d432",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0cafb6e6-6e22-4a64-81d7-1e0f7c8f3188"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa59b863-6a5b-4a2a-9670-f9f967e2f2c1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that wherever the cutoff goes, every Banger is an older song, so the year a person picks changes how lopsided the sides look but not who got labeled Banger.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of ten song cards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of three or four.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Song Piles",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "All ten songs on the cards are real, school-friendly songs from 2010 to 2025."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa59b863-6a5b-4a2a-9670-f9f967e2f2c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5ad5dde-13b8-4e4a-8d27-a1c27b8b5c0f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You have ten song cards. They are Playlist Bot A's training data. Line them up by Release Year, oldest to newest. Remember Lesson 5? You chose the cutoffs for your room tree. Here, Bot A's builder chose the year. The builder made a feature called Before 2025, so 2025 is the cutoff. Lay your pencil between the cards at 2025. Look at the cards on each side.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5ad5dde-13b8-4e4a-8d27-a1c27b8b5c0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9c53522-7482-4d95-8c30-56f2aa75e3af",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give groups a moment to look at the cards on each side of the pencil.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look at both sides of your pencil. Where are all the Bangers?*\n* Turn and Talk: partners talk for one minute, then a few groups share.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that every Banger is on one side of the pencil. Listen for: \"All eight Bangers are before 2025. Only Skips are after.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have groups slide the pencil to 2015. Six songs are now before 2015 (all Bangers) and four are from 2015 on (two Bangers, two Skips).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *You moved your pencil. What changed, and what stayed the same?*\n* *What did the person who chose 2025 believe about good dance songs?*\n* Turn and Talk: partners take one minute per question, then a few groups share with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should see that moving the pencil makes the sides closer to even but doesn't change who got labeled Banger, so the labels came from a person's choice about good dance songs. Listen for: \"The new songs are still the only Skips\" and \"They thought only older songs make good dance songs.\" If students stay vague, ask: \"Who chose 2025, and which songs did they label Banger?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define balanced data: training data with enough records of each category. Point to the balance picture."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9c53522-7482-4d95-8c30-56f2aa75e3af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6f91c0b-e250-4d57-80a3-ea7dc6e3cba8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Bot A's two sides were lopsided. Balanced data has enough records of each category, and each category has both Bangers and Skips. That gives the model records of each category to find patterns in.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6f91c0b-e250-4d57-80a3-ea7dc6e3cba8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7694da7-8cb0-411d-8d40-152da1e4dd6f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define unbalanced data: training data where records of one category show up much more often than the others. Ask the class whether Bot A's data is balanced or unbalanced."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7694da7-8cb0-411d-8d40-152da1e4dd6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bce46af-975a-495b-8eed-c4435932e1fc",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look back at your Song Piles cards. Eight of Bot A's ten records are songs from before 2025, and every Banger is an older song. That's unbalanced data.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bce46af-975a-495b-8eed-c4435932e1fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "416e7a20-fdb5-4c73-9261-a9025363389e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that two models built with the same app give different predictions when they're trained on different records.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Lesson 6 activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show this slide before the Level 1 slide. Point to the Dataset and Testing buttons in the top left corner of AI Studio.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Same App, Different Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "416e7a20-fdb5-4c73-9261-a9025363389e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d4075e0-167d-4ca8-aee8-85840fc0cc58",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI Studio has two tabs. The Dataset tab shows the records, and you can sort and filter them. The Testing tab shows the tree, and you use Make a Prediction there. A record is all the data about one song. In the table view, each record shows as one row.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d4075e0-167d-4ca8-aee8-85840fc0cc58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a7559a7-ade7-4d24-8008-8095e83c60e5",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Playlist Bot A is the school's bot. The student DJ crew used the same app to build Playlist Bot B with their own records. First, test Bot A with the new hit, Soda Pop.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a7559a7-ade7-4d24-8008-8095e83c60e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e583ffe-494e-4a64-a489-08ac96d7b598",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1. Students test Bot A with Soda Pop and find the question that led to its prediction (Before 2025, so Skip).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for pairs who click Replay before they write. When a pair finds the question, say: \"Remember Lesson 2? The Asker pointed each Traveler down one path. This question splits the songs the same way your pencil did.\"",
        "progression_name": "Same App, Different Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e583ffe-494e-4a64-a489-08ac96d7b598",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42afe7ea-a3ac-45df-b80b-b1e533a76d94",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Keep students in their pairs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42afe7ea-a3ac-45df-b80b-b1e533a76d94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d68bb688-28f1-4d11-8f3f-0c6d50ad9263",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now check Bot B's records before anyone uses Bot B.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d68bb688-28f1-4d11-8f3f-0c6d50ad9263",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15d84b60-33b0-4212-9e57-a641bd2133fc",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 2. Students sort and filter Bot B's records and decide whether they are balanced (10 records and 6 Bangers in each category, so balanced). Give pairs two minutes to figure out how to sort and filter on their own. Then show the next two slides.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who back their choice with counts. Ask: \"Which counts tell you whether Bot B's data is balanced?\"",
        "progression_name": "Same App, Different Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15d84b60-33b0-4212-9e57-a641bd2133fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0648b324-0b69-4ea4-82cb-f3231cb37cfe",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** After two minutes, bring the class back and show these steps. Point to the sort button next to Before 2025."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0648b324-0b69-4ea4-82cb-f3231cb37cfe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ad12102-94ad-4396-b139-40b6bb868b3c",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sorting puts records with the same category next to each other. It works like your pencil in Song Piles.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ad12102-94ad-4396-b139-40b6bb868b3c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2133133d-2e32-4462-9491-e682e8269cbe",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show these steps right after the sort steps. Point to Filter, the Yes checkbox, Done and the number of rows next to Playlist Bot B. Then give pairs two more minutes to finish their four counts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for pairs who forget to switch the filter to No. Ask: \"How many records are in the other category?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The lower screenshot shows Bot B's records with the Yes filter on. Send pairs back to Level 2 quickly so they find the counts on their own."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2133133d-2e32-4462-9491-e682e8269cbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7086e5c-ee26-468d-b16f-58d9f0beb7e2",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Bot A and Bot B use the same app. What is different about their records?*\n* Turn and Talk: partners talk for one minute, then two or three pairs share.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should compare what is in each bot's records. Listen for: \"Bot A is mostly older songs, and Bot B has older and new songs.\" If students say the bots are different apps, ask: \"What did the DJ crew change when they built Bot B?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 3. Students test Bot B with Soda Pop and compare its answer with Bot A's (Bot B says Banger; Beat Drop led to it).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for pairs who compare the two answers side by side. Ask: \"Which question did Bot B ask first about the new hit?\"",
        "progression_name": "Check Bot B"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7086e5c-ee26-468d-b16f-58d9f0beb7e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7e788e0-a684-4285-bf60-fa15e2fccf35",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Both bots got the same new hit. Why did they give different answers?*\n* Popcorn Share: take short answers from several pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect each answer to the records each bot was trained on. This answers the Question of the Day. A strong response sounds like: \"Bot A was trained on mostly old songs, so it predicts Skip for new ones.\" If students say a bot likes some songs, ask: \"What in its records led to that prediction?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7e788e0-a684-4285-bf60-fa15e2fccf35",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f62cd4e0-4aa8-4466-94f1-b3bfce6f961e",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that writing down a model's training data shows what is missing from it and what the model will probably predict.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Lesson 6 Model Card handout.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs fill in the Model Card for Playlist Bot A.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for pairs who circle Unbalanced and name what is missing (songs from 2025 on labeled Banger). A strong last row sounds like: \"The data has a lot of older songs and not much new music, so the model will probably predict Skip for new songs.\" Ask: \"Which songs were labeled Banger, and when did they come out?\"",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Model Card"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f62cd4e0-4aa8-4466-94f1-b3bfce6f961e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56777c71-2943-4198-8f94-39b2e3a873a3",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that a bot's Skip traces back to the records it was trained on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students choose every answer they agree with. Then ask for a show of hands for each letter.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Auditor's Check",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The correct answers are B, C and E. If most students choose those, move on. If many choose A or D, ask, \"What in Bot A's records led to the Skip?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56777c71-2943-4198-8f94-39b2e3a873a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "438c4fd7-2f08-4e2a-a488-61e3a99c1598",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask for a show of hands for each answer.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The correct answer is B. If most students choose B, move on. If many choose A or C, ask, \"Which records would Bot A be trained on now?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "438c4fd7-2f08-4e2a-a488-61e3a99c1598",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4999b952-afac-48ad-913e-4e39fbfb6415"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b6a320a-90dd-457d-81e2-a7df3ca5e0c4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work on their own. Give them three minutes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 4. Students write who Playlist Bot A might leave out at the dance.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "The Auditor's Call",
        "progression_name": "The Auditor's Call",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look for answers that name a group the bot leaves out (students who like new music) and connect it to Bot A's records (no songs from 2025 on were labeled Banger). If many answers name only a group, ask the class next lesson: \"What in Bot A's records leaves that group out?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b6a320a-90dd-457d-81e2-a7df3ca5e0c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56a2b26-7c68-4077-83ad-318a49ea0890"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08e98487-ee9b-4bf4-a666-2e88f99bf561",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08e98487-ee9b-4bf4-a666-2e88f99bf561",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56a2b26-7c68-4077-83ad-318a49ea0890"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f04a74a6-bbed-436e-a23c-e87fc129d354",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Two bots can use the same app and give different answers, because they're trained on different records. Bot A was trained mostly on older songs, and only older songs were labeled Banger, so it predicts Skip for new songs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f04a74a6-bbed-436e-a23c-e87fc129d354",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56a2b26-7c68-4077-83ad-318a49ea0890"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76fc1002-dc08-47c6-80b9-84f1806aeb95",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76fc1002-dc08-47c6-80b9-84f1806aeb95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56a2b26-7c68-4077-83ad-318a49ea0890"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9779ec7e-9452-4613-a841-bda1378f1c0e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see that a movie is a system made of many teams, and that the next three lessons are about helping those teams.\n\n**Video:** Show the \"Amazon Future Engineer\" clip from 01:39 - 02:18. It introduces film-making careers and explains that filmmaking is a system with many parts. Questions to consider with the video:\n* How is film-making like a system?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect the idea of a system to the work ahead: many teams, each making decisions that affect the others. Listen for students naming parts beyond the actors and director, such as the visual effects artists, sound designers, engineers, and computer scientists from the video. If answers stay at \"lots of people work on movies,\" ask: What happens to the rest of the movie if one team makes a bad call?\n\n**Say**: For the next three lessons, you're going to be acting in the role of a data scientist working with teams behind the camera.\n\n**Say**: Clint's team builds a 3D rough draft of a scene before anyone films it. In movies, that's called pre-visualization.\n\n**Video:** Show the \"Pre-visualization\" clip from 00:55-01:27. Questions to consider with the video:\n\n* If you could ask only ONE question about a scene to decide, what would you ask?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Each question a student shares is a feature. Listen for questions about what makes a scene hard or dangerous, like \"Is there a stunt?\" or \"Does something crash?\" Also listen for questions about how it looks or feels, like \"Is it scary?\" Students will get to test some of these features in AI Studio.\n\n**Say**: Let's hear how Clint's team decides which scenes get a rough draft.\n\n**Video:** Show the \"Which Scenes Get Chosen?\" clip from 03:00-03:21. Questions to consider with the video:\n\n* Did your question match what the pre-visualization team looks for?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that an expert decides by looking at several things at once: how complex the scene is, how much tech is involved, safety, and whether you only get one chance. That's the reason a decision tree asks more than one question. Some students may catch \"only one chance to get it right\" which will turn out to be a strong feature that helps guide decision trees that they will create today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9779ec7e-9452-4613-a841-bda1378f1c0e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b0deffae-3194-4080-867f-975514e5b0c4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9793ca6b-c9c8-4bb2-bc4d-cbc6619f7a65",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student/group a copy of the Train Your Decision Tree activity guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9793ca6b-c9c8-4bb2-bc4d-cbc6619f7a65",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb3934a5-6c3e-4610-874e-94e65d71db45",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students read past scenes and guess what features the pre-visualization team looked at when deciding whether to Just Film It or Create a Rough Draft.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of three for this activity so that they can compare and contrast their unique trees.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1. Students browse the dataset in Cards view. Have them use the Filter option to different views of the data. Have them predict which features seem most important based on their reading of the data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Many students will read only the scene description and skip the features listed. Ask them which features on the card back up their guess. A few students may notice that the word \"real\" shows up in a lot of descriptions. Don't confirm or explain it; ask them to keep an eye on it.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Meet the Data",
        "progression_name": "Meet the Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb3934a5-6c3e-4610-874e-94e65d71db45",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5964b9b-cad5-4ea4-8bc9-965021177c0f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What observations did you make about the scene dataset?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share the guesses they wrote in Level 1. Some will point to action, like stunts and crashes. Others will point to feeling, like scary or serious scenes. Acknowledge both kinds and don't settle which is right; the next levels test them. If a student brings up the scene descriptions, ask: If you couldn't read the description, which features would you look at?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5964b9b-cad5-4ea4-8bc9-965021177c0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "527ad147-af0b-49e9-920c-a64e79dabfa6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students train two trees on the same scenes using different features. They compare the results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 2 to train their first decision tree using the Mostly Talking, Mood, and Indoors or Outdoors features. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that Predict says Decision before students train. Then check that each tree uses only the three features listed in the activity guide. Students often leave extra features checked from the first tree when they train the second one.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Training Decision Trees",
        "progression_name": "Training Decision Trees",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Once students have trained the tree using the features in the Tree 1 row of their Activity Guide, return to the slides in order to walk through the next steps together as guided practice. They will have the opportunity to train a second tree using the skills that they learn in the walkthrough."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "527ad147-af0b-49e9-920c-a64e79dabfa6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c8156a7-a07d-4cac-a97f-52342b19638f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to the numbers on each leaf. Each check mark is a test scene the tree got right, and each X is one it got wrong. Count the checks together to show where the accuracy number comes from.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students click one of the end nodes on the decision tree in order to bring up a breakdown of the data that got filtered into it. Have them experiment with toggling between correct and incorrect predictions.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "There are a lot of options within the scorecard which can be confusing for now. Just have students focus on looking at the correct / incorrect predictions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c8156a7-a07d-4cac-a97f-52342b19638f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c613cdd-987b-493c-8a90-8225260a0d80",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students where the Accuracy button is, and have them record one correct and one incorrect test scene in the activity guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "While the decision nodes contained information only about testing / training data that got sorted into that branch of the decision tree, the Scorecard contains information about how all of the test data performed within the decision tree."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c613cdd-987b-493c-8a90-8225260a0d80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b483896-b9ea-4c15-93f9-382028f6c0ec",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once students have recorded the accuracy number and examples for their first decision tree, have them click the Start over button to train a second tree. The features to use are listed in the Tree 2 row in the students' activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Both trees learned from the same scenes. Why did one do better?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The point here is for students to see that the data didn't change, only the features did. Many will start with \"the second tree had better features.\" Ask what makes them better for this team's job. Students should notice that the second tree's features describe what makes a scene hard or risky to film, while the first tree's describe how a scene looks or feels. Connect this back to the warm-up by asking: Which of these features match what Clint said his team looks for? If a student says the tree should just read the scene description, point out that the tree never sees the description, only the features."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b483896-b9ea-4c15-93f9-382028f6c0ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f7bb7a3-170e-41d7-bca6-e1e48a004a91",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students try other combinations of features and test whether adding more features makes a better tree.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 3. Students train several trees using different combinations of features and use the All results button to compare them. Ask them to check whether adding more features actually makes a tree better.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Training Different Features",
        "progression_name": "Training Different Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f7bb7a3-170e-41d7-bca6-e1e48a004a91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b2cc690-50e2-4d6b-b314-c722ac5c18e4",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who select all six features and stop. Ask them to find a smaller set that does just as well. If a student is stuck below 75%, ask which features describe what makes a scene hard to film.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** [Think-Pair-Share] Were there any features that did not help when you trained your tree?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students find that adding features doesn't always make a better tree. Adding Mood or Indoors or Outdoors to the three action features rarely raises the score, and sometimes the tree just ignores them. When students argue over a few points, remind them the score comes from only 8 test scenes. Then ask: Which tree would you trust with a scene it has never seen?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b2cc690-50e2-4d6b-b314-c722ac5c18e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c6a9fba-e7e6-405f-9c85-ba5ea9d4a5fd",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use their tree to make a prediction for a new scene and watch the path it takes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 4. Students invent their own scene in their activity guide, then enter a scene's features into the Make a Prediction box, press Predict, then analyze how the tree sorts it.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Invent a Scene",
        "progression_name": "Invent a Scene"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c6a9fba-e7e6-405f-9c85-ba5ea9d4a5fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f20e8213-cc0e-4d10-b16c-71c93ef84951",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Steer students away from the Random button. It creates scenes that make no sense, like an indoor scene that is mostly talking while something gets destroyed. Ask students to describe a real scene from a movie they know and enter that instead."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f20e8213-cc0e-4d10-b16c-71c93ef84951",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e8b0a3d-918a-4f82-b6ec-5d1abb3c4087",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Each students selects a new feature to add to the same base data. They compare how one added feature changes a tree and test it using one or more of the challenge scenes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Each students chooses one of the different features to add to the dataset: Movie Budget, Famous Actor, or Can It Be Filmed Again.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 5. Students choose their assigned feature and train a tree with their best features from Level 3 plus the new one.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Add a Feature",
        "progression_name": "Add a Feature",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In a group of two, each student tests one feature. In a group of four, two students can share a feature."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e8b0a3d-918a-4f82-b6ec-5d1abb3c4087",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fccaf5f-fc88-4ce0-9698-a80e2250dc58",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Point out to students that the new feature also shows up in the Predict menu. Make sure Predict (label) still says Decision and that students didn't change that accidentally when updating features.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Did your new tree disagree on any Challenge Scenes? Why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Most teams will find their trees disagree on the car crush. Push students to trace the answer back to the data: in past small-budget movies, risky scenes like this one were often just filmed. The tree learned what past teams decided, not what each scene needed. When students say the data shows small movies don't plan, ask: Is that true about filmmaking, or true about these 79 scenes?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fccaf5f-fc88-4ce0-9698-a80e2250dc58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "982513d0-d281-4318-9c5e-c0adefeb4a87"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9cb67b42-d373-460e-a6ba-a0a5a48d1dc2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students name an assumption built into the training data, back it up with something they saw today, and suggest a feature that could help.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 6 to answer the Check for Understanding free response question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If a student is stuck on the first blank, ask them to think back to a Challenge Scene where their team's trees disagreed. Ask: Why might past movie teams have made that call?\n",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look for two things in student responses:\n\n* Does the student name an assumption, not just a problem? Strong answers say something about real movies that the data takes for granted, and back it up with a scene or tree from today. For example: \"Small-budget movies don't need to plan risky scenes. I think this because the Movie Budget tree said Just Film It for the car crush.\" Answers about the data being \"bad\" or the tree being \"wrong\" show the student sees a problem but hasn't yet named what the data assumes. Ask them: What did past movie teams believe when they made that decision?\n\n* Does the feature connect to the assumption? Strong answers name a feature and say why it would help, such as \"Can It Be Filmed Again, because a dam leap only happens once.\" Naming a feature without a reason is a good start. A feature with no link to the assumption, like \"Mood,\" suggests the student is guessing.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9cb67b42-d373-460e-a6ba-a0a5a48d1dc2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3d413f2-2566-462c-9e8b-5ac25d12866c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0d1bcc5-43e2-4d3b-8b95-677f6d2ca890",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0d1bcc5-43e2-4d3b-8b95-677f6d2ca890",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3d413f2-2566-462c-9e8b-5ac25d12866c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b191810-a1a3-4f6f-bf20-c9e50f51c583",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b191810-a1a3-4f6f-bf20-c9e50f51c583",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f3d413f2-2566-462c-9e8b-5ac25d12866c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3e89a807-4b4b-4519-900e-0f3fcc9fbecd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: Last lesson, you helped the rough draft team plan scenes before filming. Today you're working with a team that comes in later, once there are clips to share.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students make a quick prediction and explain their reasons. Their reasons are features they'll see in today's data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Have students vote with a show of hands, then call on a few to explain.\n\n* Which clip will more people like? Why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The vote matters less than the reasons behind it. Students will say things like \"it's funny,\" \"it's short,\" or \"a famous actor is in it.\" If the class splits, ask: How could the marketing team find out who was right?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How does the digital marketing team decide which clips to use?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Catherine works like a decision tree: she looks at how past clips did and makes a rule, like \"funny bloopers beat trailers, so post more funny clips.\" Students may suggest that a new kind of clip could get popular, or that something could happen that the numbers don't show. Students will find real examples in the data today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3e89a807-4b4b-4519-900e-0f3fcc9fbecd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2bdeb689-5338-49f0-8267-9c2788828703"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7cea9f01-a432-400f-ad7b-458df2ee4063",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student/group a copy of the Testing and Tracing Predictions activity guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7cea9f01-a432-400f-ad7b-458df2ee4063",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2d7feb1-cc7d-4efa-b101-3fe78470d0b9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students get to know the marketing data and examine how test data using the model performs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 8, Levels 1 - 2:\n- **Level 1:** Students browse the clips in Cards view and write one pattern they notice in their activity guide.\n- **Level 2:** Students examine a trained tree and uncover which test items were incorrectly predicted.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Level 1, ask students which reasons provided in the warm-up video show up as features on the cards. Some will notice that most hits have a famous actor, or that behind-the-scenes clips are rare. On Level 2, check that Predict says Result and that only the four listed features are selected. Every student should see 66.67%. If a student's number is different, check which features they used to train the model.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Testing a Decision Tree",
        "progression_name": "Testing a Decision Tree",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The next slide will walk through the steps that students should perform to see the incorrectly labeled data more explicitly."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2d7feb1-cc7d-4efa-b101-3fe78470d0b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28dd3ff2-528a-4997-9713-bf08cd53774f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Model these steps that can be used to find the model's mistakes. Point out that the tree tested itself on 9 clips it never trained on, and these are the ones it got wrong.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Expand columns shows the clip's description and every feature, even ones the tree didn't train on. Students will need this skill in order to trace incorrect predictions back to data."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28dd3ff2-528a-4997-9713-bf08cd53774f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1559109b-2091-40de-8ce2-f39ebb84e2f6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which wrong clip surprises you most?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This question gives students a reason to trace. Most guesses will be about the clips themselves, like \"the tree doesn't know dances are funny.\" Collect two or three guesses without judging them. If students start blaming the computer, ask: Where did the tree learn its rules in the first place? That's the question the next part of the lesson answers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define tracing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define test data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1559109b-2091-40de-8ce2-f39ebb84e2f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12bf2694-df6f-4abb-91fb-30a42cd936ab",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice how to trace from prediction back through a decision tree to the original data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 3. Students follow along on their own screens as you model each step of the tracing process.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Trace a Mistake",
        "progression_name": "Trace a Mistake",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The following steps describe the tracing process:\n\n1. What did it predict? Enter the clip and press Predict.\n\n2. What path did it take? Press Replay and follow the questions.\n\n3. What clips taught it that? Check the Training rows, then filter the Dataset.\n\nNote: Long labels get cut off, like \"Behind the Sc...\". Show students that they can hover over it to read the full label so they don't skip a step."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12bf2694-df6f-4abb-91fb-30a42cd936ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cf45f50-347c-4c61-8b18-a1d558e1923a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students should enter the following features:\n\n* Clip Type: Behind the Scenes\n* Length: Short\n* Famous Actor: Yes\n* Funny: Yes\n\n**Say:** Refer to the Trace a Mistake section in your activity guide and enter in the feature values that you see for Level 3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how to click the replay button to go through the questions that the decision tree asks in order to sort the data. Record these in the Activity Guide.\n\n**Say:** Let's watch the path that the data travels to get to the prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how to click the decision node, and how to switch from Test rows to Training rows. Click Expand columns to the read the four clips aloud. All four are behind-the-scenes clips, and all four were Meh.\n\n**Say:** These four clips seem to be the reason the tree predicted \"Meh\" for our test data– it's doing what the training clips taught it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how to navigate back to the Dataset and how to filter the data for Behind the Scenes clips. Together with the students, count the clips that are filtered. There are 9 behind-the-scenes clips. Eight were \"Meh\", and the dance clip is the only \"Hit\".",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The filter allows one condition at a time. Pick the feature that matters most for the clip you're tracing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cf45f50-347c-4c61-8b18-a1d558e1923a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef9e327f-8e6f-4929-911a-f562bdd6b9fd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** The tree's rule says Behind the Scenes clips are \"Meh\". Where did that rule come from?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should come away seeing that the tree followed its training data exactly. It never saw a behind-the-scenes clip that was a hit, so it had no way to predict one. When students say \"the tree is bad,\" push them to describe what the tree would need to see to get this clip right. This is the first kind of mistake: the data didn't have enough examples. Connect it back to Catherine's video and the idea that patterns can go out of date.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 4. Pairs follow the same three steps and fill in their second trace card.",
        "progression_name": "Trace Another Mistake"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef9e327f-8e6f-4929-911a-f562bdd6b9fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b8ae129-9078-4aba-be80-7364ffd995ee",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Pairs might get stuck here because every step on the path makes sense. Tell them to look at the long interviews that aren't funny. Which ones were hits? Only the premiere clip was. In this case, the description \"on premiere night\" might explains why it was misclassified. Since this is not one of the features that the dataset is using, it shows a possible issue with the data features that were curated and therefore the way that the tree was trained.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**\n\n* Did the tree do anything wrong?\n* What was missing?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This mistake is different from the dance clip. The tree followed its data, and similar interviews really were Meh. The data never recorded the thing that made this clip a hit. Push students past \"the tree needs more interviews\" by asking: If we added ten more interviews, would the tree know about premiere night? Students should arrive at the idea that the data is missing a feature."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b8ae129-9078-4aba-be80-7364ffd995ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea70e57d-bf4a-4b23-8cb1-95ae6bc72017",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students change the training data, retrain, and see whether a prediction changes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 5. Have students choose one of the proposed changes to the clips that the traced earlier. They write a prediction in the activity guide, train with the appropriate features, and analyze the result..\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Make sure students write their prediction before they train. Students who add hits or remove flops will see the Silly Dance clip leave the Incorrect tab, and the accuracy will rise to 77.78%. Students who add interviews for the Premiere Night clip will see nothing change, and the accuracy will stay at 66.67%. Ask those students: Why didn't four more interviews help?",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Test a Fix",
        "progression_name": "Test a Fix",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "All three datasets keep the same 9 test clips, so any change in accuracy comes from the training data, not from different test clips."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea70e57d-bf4a-4b23-8cb1-95ae6bc72017",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93686380-88d8-4d93-8377-648ad477dfca",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which mistakes could changing the data fix? Which couldn't?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect the results back to the two kinds of mistakes discussed throughout this lesson. Adding or removing examples fixed the dance clip, which was missing examples. No number of new interviews fixed the premiere clip, because the reason it was a hit was never a feature. The second question has no single right answer. Students who prefer adding hits often say removing data throws away information; students who prefer removing flops say old clips don't reflect what people like now. Press both sides to explain what each change does to the tree."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93686380-88d8-4d93-8377-648ad477dfca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e20dffde-67f9-487d-8a5e-bb1ec392e307"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5dd31760-0a6e-48b2-83c0-449196b8957d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 6. Students answer on their own.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Strong answers name a specific prediction and the data that caused it. For example: \"The tree called the dance clip Meh because every behind-the-scenes clip in the training data was Meh. It never saw one that was a hit.\" Some students will use the premiere-night interview instead: \"Every long interview in the data was Meh, and nothing in the data said it was premiere night.\" Both work. Answers like \"the data was bad\" or \"it didn't have enough data\" show a student who knows data matters but hasn't connected it to a specific mistake yet. Ask them: Which clips in the training data taught the tree that answer?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5dd31760-0a6e-48b2-83c0-449196b8957d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bdf37acc-8483-4403-ae76-35d9bb256994"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e808aa9-88fd-4f43-9454-37224768eaed",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How would you answer the Question of the Day now?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Answers should point to something students traced today: missing examples, or a feature the data never recorded. If someone answers \"the computer messed up,\" ask which step in their trace shows that. Every step in today's traces followed the training data exactly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e808aa9-88fd-4f43-9454-37224768eaed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bdf37acc-8483-4403-ae76-35d9bb256994"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "caa6623e-db56-4050-8f82-970edbc86d08",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: Last lesson, you helped the digital marketing team. Today, you're working with a team that chooses whether a scene should be filmed in front of an LED screen or on location.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students meet the last team in the Amazon Studios career sequence and list the reasons a scene gets filmed in front of the LED wall.\n\n**Say**: You've helped the rough draft team plan scenes and the marketing team predict hits. Today's team decides where scenes get filmed: on a real location, or in front of a giant LED wall that can show any place on Earth or off it.\n\n**Video:** Show the \"Virtual Production\" clip (Video 3) from 02:20–03:03. Mike and Chewy explain why the team uses the LED wall.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What reasons did the team give for using the LED wall instead of a real location?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** List the reasons on the board as students call them out: the place is far away, travel is expensive, the place doesn't exist, the scene is dangerous to film for real, or the actors need to see where they are. Students already know a feature is something a model looks at, so ask: Which of these could be features? All of them. Leave the list up; students will start combining them later in the lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "caa6623e-db56-4050-8f82-970edbc86d08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a0f6d272-423e-4798-93fd-daee1d56cb50"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "919b5a5f-92f0-4019-b0de-898c06c622d4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Combining Features activity guide, and each pair a card set and a printed copy of the base model's decision tree.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into pairs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "919b5a5f-92f0-4019-b0de-898c06c622d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5778990-8bc9-4028-a25a-7aded5903b3c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students combine features by hand and test their combination on real scenes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students examine the Virtual Production decision tree model, circling the two questions on the tree that they find most similar. Then, have them sort their scene cards using the decision tree, assigning each scene the label \"Real location\" or \"LED Wall\". The correct label is on the back of the cards so that students can check this test set for accuracy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Most pairs will circle Far Away and Expensive to Travel, which sit right next to each other on one branch. When they sort the cards with the tree, they'll miss the two scenes at sunset and sunrise. Ask: What question would the tree need to get those right?",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Feature Combination",
        "progression_name": "What is Feature Combination?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The printed tree gets 6 of 8 cards right. It misses card 1 (sunset) and card 8 (sunrise), because it never asks about time of day."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5778990-8bc9-4028-a25a-7aded5903b3c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d49aad61-43c1-4277-a638-b1ab59b544bf",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How accurate was this decision tree model?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Most pairs will get 6 out of 8. Have a pair name the two cards the tree missed, then ask what they have in common. Students should notice that both depend on time of day, and the tree never asks about it, because it ran out of room. Don't fix it yet; that's what combining features is for.\n\n**Say:** We can combine several features into one feature to make our models simpler and more accurate. We can combine features in multiple different ways.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the idea that features can be combined using two different joining words, either or both:\n* **Either -** Ask students what is a new feature that we might create if a player in a video game is either \"Out of lives\" or \"Out of time\". One possible answer might be a new feature called \"Game Over\".\n* **Both -** Ask students what is a new feature that we might create if it was both hot outside and the pool was open. One possible answer might be new features called \"Pool Day\" or \"Summer Vacation\".\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell pairs that they can review the features for our Virtual Production decision tree model on the back of the handout. Give pairs time to choose two or more features and a joiner word, and then fill out the Round 2 section in their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** The joiner is the hardest choice. If a pair picks Both, ask: Is it really a problem only when both are true, or when either one is?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give pairs time to sort. Have them flip their cards to reveal how many were correct based on their new feature. Students should then record this accuracy number in their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students to sort using only the new feature that they developed. If their accuracy is poor, ask them to consider what they might change to make their accuracy better. Remind students that even low accuracy results are useful as it reveals information about the data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Call on two or three pairs to pitch to you as the virtual production team. Include at least one pair whose combination scored low.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Sentence Stem: \"Our feature ___ combines ___ and ___. It got ___ / 8 right, which is better / worse because ___.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d49aad61-43c1-4277-a638-b1ab59b544bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3be26820-8b1f-48cc-bd58-c3b391d72b73",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1, the four-panel level. It shows the same idea students just used, with a recess example: two questions that ask about the same thing, combined into one new feature, \"Wet outside?\", that makes the tree shorter.",
        "progression_name": "Combining Features",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this short. Students already did the hard thinking with the cards; the panels give it a picture and a name."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3be26820-8b1f-48cc-bd58-c3b391d72b73",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b70db9d5-1661-4d1d-9391-2adddb6fbaa2",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define combined feature.\n\n**Say:** What you just made has a name: a combined feature. Your \"Wet outside?\" or \"Can't Control the Sky\" is one question doing the work of two."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b70db9d5-1661-4d1d-9391-2adddb6fbaa2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cd28a00-0574-4a20-8cf9-62e9ee148fa5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use AI Studio to compare how combining features affects accuracy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 3.\n- **Level 2:** Students train with all seven original features and record the accuracy (88.89%).\n- **Level 3:** This dataset includes four combined features: Hard to Get To, Can't Control the Sky, Trouble Count, and Busy Night. Students remove the features they combined, add their combined feature, train, and record the accuracy.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Test Your Combined Feature",
        "progression_name": "Test Your Combined Feature"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cd28a00-0574-4a20-8cf9-62e9ee148fa5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fad7a93-3bfa-47cc-ae29-19a1638de703",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Make sure students swap features rather than just add them. If the original features stay selected, the tree may keep using them. Pairs whose combination isn't one of the four test the closest one.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For Level 3, specifically, remind students that they can click the feature column in AI Studio in order to read a description of the combined features.\n\nThe expected accuracy: Hard to Get To, Can't Control the Sky, or Trouble Count, 100%; Busy Night, 88.89% (the tree ignores it)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fad7a93-3bfa-47cc-ae29-19a1638de703",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1021e93d-2f6a-47d6-9d08-ceea0d4316ed",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students export their Level 3 model, the one with the combined feature, to the Backpack. They'll import it into the model card.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Did using a combined feature help the accuracy of your model?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Most students will see accuracy jump from about 89% to 100%. One combined question frees up room for the tree to check something else. Ask: Which questions disappeared from your tree? Students who tested Busy Night will find nothing changed, because the tree didn't use it. That's the point: a combination only helps when its features describe the same problem."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1021e93d-2f6a-47d6-9d08-ceea0d4316ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "868ddd07-bb51-4cd0-a4e8-e7e28201da91",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students document their model, including its strengths, its limits, and who it might fail.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define model card.\n\n**Say:** Real AI teams write a model card for every model they build, so anyone who uses it knows what it's good at and where it might go wrong. Part of yours is already filled in. You'll add your combined feature, your tree, and its strengths and limits.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 4. The Sketch Lab model card already has The Basics, Features, and Who It's For filled in. Students complete three things:\n* Features: \"I combined ___ and ___ into ___. Accuracy went from ___% to ___%.\"\n* How It Decides: import their tree from the Backpack and annotate it with the shape tool.\n* Strengths & Limits: what it does well, and who it might fail and why.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Complete A Model Card",
        "progression_name": "Complete A Model Card"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "868ddd07-bb51-4cd0-a4e8-e7e28201da91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e98ec50b-bcee-4a4a-b4c3-844b32e535e1",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Make sure to point out the pencil icons which represent places where the students are expected to complete sections of their model card.",
        "progression_name": "Test Feature Combination",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The Strengths & Limits panel shows whether students can describe who a model might fail. Strong answers name a specific kind of scene or production, like \"small movies that need a sunset shot close to home,\" and connect it to something in the tree or data. Answers like \"it might be wrong sometimes\" show a student who knows models fail but hasn't named who. Ask: Which branch would send that scene the wrong way?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e98ec50b-bcee-4a4a-b4c3-844b32e535e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83393fab-ca6c-4092-a833-bda1be92252f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Whose scenes might your model get wrong?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students name a specific kind of scene or production their model could get wrong and connect it to a branch on their tree. If a student says \"It might be wrong sometimes,\" ask, \"Whose movie would that be?\"",
        "progression_name": "Complete A Model Card"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83393fab-ca6c-4092-a833-bda1be92252f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "708f020e-d440-4605-a261-bfbf36f5d658"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0f59274-1a2e-44ac-acc2-48251ac3920d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How would you answer the Question of the Day now?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Look for answers that point to something students tested today, like Hard to Get To fixing the gas station scene, or Busy Night scoring worse than guessing on paper. If someone just says \"yes,\" ask: What makes a question better?\n\n*Say:** Over the last three lessons, you've worked with three teams behind the camera: the rough draft team, the marketing team, and the virtual production team. Each one makes better decisions when someone thinks carefully about the data, which is exactly what you've been doing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0f59274-1a2e-44ac-acc2-48251ac3920d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6bcd3c40-d4b2-429d-b423-5b02913116af"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16e80df6-d4a8-4837-b294-f58fbd225f8f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see a model score high on their own class. Then the students it got wrong say what that felt like. That sets up the question the audit will answer: who is in the leftover percent, and why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show each prediction. Count the students standing, and have them sit before the next one\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have the class calculate the accuracy for one prediction and write it in the blank.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If no one in the class is left-handed, hopefully the birthday prediction will still have some students sitting. If not, consider your own unique question that will have just a few students sitting."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16e80df6-d4a8-4837-b294-f58fbd225f8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5446c81e-73da-41c6-8173-b033f3ea9983"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "594bacf0-7d8e-494d-9b36-28f5ecd1396c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n\n* Is this a good model?\n\n* Has anything ever been designed in a way that didn't work for you?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Most of the class will call the model good, and that's the belief you want students holding when they start the audit. Don't argue with it yet. The second question usually brings out left-handed students talking about scissors, spiral notebooks, desks, or game controllers. When that comes up, ask whether those things work for most people, and let the room see that \"most people\" and \"everyone\" aren't the same. Leave it there. Who gets left out of a real app, and why, is the work students do today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "594bacf0-7d8e-494d-9b36-28f5ecd1396c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5446c81e-73da-41c6-8173-b033f3ea9983"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f027d8d7-bb31-430d-a1d5-b7906c20acb0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student/group a copy of the Accuracy ≠ Fairness activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students write a one-sentence first answer to the Question of the Day at the top of their activity guide. They'll come back to it at the end of class."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f027d8d7-bb31-430d-a1d5-b7906c20acb0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd423ce6-9ede-42f1-8e7f-c0d0612782e2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students choose a case file, write a first answer to the Question of the Day, and choose a case to investigate\n\n**Say:** A few minutes ago, our model was right about most of you, and a few of you sat down. These three students sent in complaints about apps that keep getting things wrong for them. Your job is to figure out whether it's a one-time glitch or something that keeps happening to people like them, and why.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 2.\n- **Level 1:** Students read all three complaints, choose one case, and write the complaint in their own words in their activity guide.\n- **Level 2:** Students open the app for the case which they chose. Students try it and record what happened.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Stakeholder Complaints & Apps",
        "progression_name": "Stakeholder Complaints & Apps"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd423ce6-9ede-42f1-8e7f-c0d0612782e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8bdd8104-3525-4f82-b90b-2815c6a26616",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For Level 1, push students to try more than one option. Have them log several kinds of days, play the week more than once, or switch lunch profiles. Ask: Does it go wrong every time, or only for some choices? For Level 2, encourage students to try more than one option. Have them log several kinds of days, play the week more than once, or switch lunch profiles. Ask: Does it go wrong every time, or only for some choices?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Spread students across the three cases so each one has at least a few investigators. The synthesis at the end of the lesson compares all three. None of the apps explain why they made a decision. That's on purpose as students find the reason by exploring the data in the next section of the lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8bdd8104-3525-4f82-b90b-2815c6a26616",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94e6b146-7a6e-47e7-8b07-15c93c60ae78",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define stakeholder\n\n**Say:** When you used the app, you were in the student's shoes. Marisol, Andre, and Jonah didn't build these apps, but the apps affect them every day. Anyone affected by what a model does is called a stakeholder.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Who else might the app affect?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This question stretches \"stakeholder\" past the one student in each complaint. Students will name the obvious person first. Push for others: Andre's mom gets the emails, other swimmers and wheelchair athletes use Marisol's app, and the cafeteria staff plans menus from Jonah's survey. Ask: Who might the app be failing that hasn't complained yet? That question leads straight into the investigation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Model each move for students, if able. Students used the first two in Lesson 8, so keep those quick and spend most of the time on the Data Card. Every case's About This Data note holds a clue.",
        "progression_name": "Student Complaint Forms",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The filter only takes one condition at a time. If students need two, have them filter by the first, then scan the results for the second. Long branch labels get cut off, like \"Bean and chees...\"; show students how to hover over and read the full label."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94e6b146-7a6e-47e7-8b07-15c93c60ae78",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca95c728-ef3f-4fe3-8894-096229cb7674",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students find out who else the app fails and trace the pattern back to a decision someone made.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 3 to train the model using the features the app uses in order to investigate its fairness.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Investigate the Model",
        "progression_name": "Investigate the Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca95c728-ef3f-4fe3-8894-096229cb7674",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5ac2902-3362-430e-bb63-e9353b48decf",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Every case follows the same path: filter to find the group, read the tree, read About this data. If a student is stuck on Step 3, point them to the feature their app leaves out:\n\n* Marisol: filter by Activity Type and look at the swimming days.\n\n* Andre: filter by Finished All Work in Gradebook: Yes, and count how many are labeled Needs Check-in.\n\n* Jonah: filter by Diet, then by Lunch Source.\n\nIn Step 4, many students stop at the tree, saying something like \"it asks about steps.\" Push them back one more step: Who decided that's what the app should look at? For Andre, ask what the Submit Time question means for a student who finished at 10 PM. For Jonah, ask why the tree never asks about Diet.",
        "progression_name": "Investigate the Model",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Each case has a known accuracy: Marisol 85.71%, Andre 100%, Jonah 88.89%. A student with a different number picked different features."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Step 4 in the activity guide is the clearest view of where students are. A student who is still describing will write what happened, like \"the app called her swim days Rest Days.\" A student who is tracing will name a decision a person made and who it hurts, like \"the app only looks at steps, so a swim practice looks the same as a day on the couch.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5ac2902-3362-430e-bb63-e9353b48decf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72223b62-add4-44a4-9536-5e08d885b015",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define audit.\n\n**Say:** What you just did has a name. You started with one person's complaint, checked whether it kept happening to the same group, and traced it back to where it started. At real tech companies, people are hired to do exactly this, and it's called an audit, or an algorithm audit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How can an app be accurate and still be unfair?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Start with a student from Andre's case. They can explain that the model followed the On Track rule perfectly, but the rule was the problem. Accuracy only measures whether a model matches its labels, not whether those labels are fair. Then bring in the other two cases to show that unfairness starts in different places: Marisol's app looks at the wrong things, and Jonah's app left people out of the data. If students land on \"the app is broken,\" push back: Did the model make any mistakes by its own rules?\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72223b62-add4-44a4-9536-5e08d885b015",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62c0ea3b-c7b2-46cc-8b53-ee3891e51997",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explain their investigation to the person it affected: how the model decides, why it failed them, and the decision behind it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 4. Students write their letter as a free response. The sentence frame is there for anyone who wants it; students who'd rather write in their own words can.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Respond to the Complaint",
        "progression_name": "Respond to the User Complaint",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "A strong letter explains the model in plain words, names what made it unfair, and ties that to a decision a person made. For example: \"The app decides if your day was active by looking at your steps. Swimming doesn't add steps, so it called your practice a Rest Day. The people who built it decided to only use what a step counter can measure.\" Letters that only apologize or repeat the complaint (\"the app was unfair to you and that's not okay\") show a student who saw the problem but hasn't explained it yet. Ask them: What does the model look at, and who decided that? Students who suggest a fix that matches the cause, like adding Activity Type for Marisol or changing the On Track rule for Andre, are going beyond today's goal."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62c0ea3b-c7b2-46cc-8b53-ee3891e51997",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec63fe53-5d8d-4986-8231-c588fb13f038"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8c30dd0-be20-4f79-ad6c-c05cc85a5159",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 5. Students study the flashcards, then match each word to its definition.",
        "progression_name": "Vocabulary Flashcards"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8c30dd0-be20-4f79-ad6c-c05cc85a5159",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98535489-39a2-4c0d-8eab-5b24853bbe43"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6183428-75a8-4919-8012-55164ef5c398",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day and give students a moment to just think about what they learned today.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Look at the answer you wrote at the top of your activity guide. Did it change?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Look for answers that point to a real person and the decision behind the problem, not a general \"it's unfair.\" A strong answer often goes back to the warm-up. For example, the model was right about most of the class, but it was always wrong about the same students, just like the apps in today's cases. If a student says \"no,\" ask who it isn't good enough for and what someone would need to change. Take two responses, ideally from different cases.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6183428-75a8-4919-8012-55164ef5c398",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98535489-39a2-4c0d-8eab-5b24853bbe43"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58735059-e89a-4d29-97af-b7836939f1d0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58735059-e89a-4d29-97af-b7836939f1d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "98535489-39a2-4c0d-8eab-5b24853bbe43"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efdbcd6a-f6b5-40d5-b026-0af10b318294",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the slide. Give students 20 seconds to recall the app they investigated in Lesson 10.\n\n**Say:** In Lesson 10, you investigated one of these apps. Marisol's fitness app kept calling her swim practice a Rest Day. Andre's homework tracker kept telling his mom he needed a check-in. Jonah's cafeteria app kept suggesting lunches he can't eat. Think about the app you studied and what you found out about it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the slide and give students 30 seconds to think.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Think about your Lesson 10 app. Who is most responsible for its unfair result? A. The model itself B. The people who collected the data C. The people who built the app D. The school that chose to use it\n\nFour Corners: Students move to the corner that matches their answer. One student from each corner explains the choice.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect their Lesson 10 app's unfair result to people who could be responsible. Every corner can be a reasonable answer, and responsibility may be shared. Listen for reasons that name a choice, like who was in the records or which features the app used. If a student gives no reason, ask: \"Which record or feature in your app led you to that corner?\" Students might begin to see that one unfair result can have several people behind it, and that people who look at the same app can reasonably blame different people. Listen for: \"People chose the records the model was trained on.\" If students say the model is to blame, ask: \"Who chose what the model was trained on, and does that change your answer?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Four Corners: post a sign for A, B, C and D in the four corners of the room. Students who can't decide can stand between two corners and explain why. If moving isn't possible, students hold up the letter with their fingers. Students stay in their corners for the next slide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efdbcd6a-f6b5-40d5-b026-0af10b318294",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7848dae-10ac-41a1-af2a-33c4ab2b5735"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "759f4391-60c4-4850-8396-f63e65831f94",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "759f4391-60c4-4850-8396-f63e65831f94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7848dae-10ac-41a1-af2a-33c4ab2b5735"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55574aab-7e5e-4371-b52d-64f1710959b7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Say**: In this video, you'll meet Dan Rosen. Dan works in responsible AI risk and policy. His job is to ask whether a system is fair before people get hurt. As you watch, think about today's question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the \"Is the System Fair?\" video. Start at 1:40 and end at 2:45.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What makes an AI system responsible and fair?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should hear the three questions Dan's team asks about an AI system: Is the system fair? Who benefits? Who could be harmed?\n\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Is the System Fair?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Videos are used throughout the curriculum to spark discussions, supplement key concepts with additional explanations and examples, and expose students to the various roles and backgrounds of individuals in computer science. While interacting with the video, turn on closed captioning so students can also read along as they watch. To encourage active engagement and reflection, use one or more of the strategies discussed in the Guide to Curriculum Videos."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55574aab-7e5e-4371-b52d-64f1710959b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaa30c09-48a8-4339-9aa3-4711b7a9d18e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "33378e85-210f-4ee6-9197-6e81c1a0c7d4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define ethics.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** [Turn and Talk] Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n\nDan Rosen said the music app is \"not trying to ignore your tastes.\" If no one meant to leave people out, who is responsible?\n\nHow is the music app like Playlist Bot at the dance?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should see that an app can leave people out even when no one meant to, and that people can disagree about who is responsible. Listen for different answers, such as the people who chose the training songs, the company that made the app, or several people at once. If students say the app is mean or broken, ask: \"What was it trained on, and who chose that?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define bias."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "33378e85-210f-4ee6-9197-6e81c1a0c7d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaa30c09-48a8-4339-9aa3-4711b7a9d18e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0872ecaa-f79e-4119-9f14-1e6f228a30c6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that unfair results from real AI systems can be traced to people's choices about records, features and labels.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in pairs. Go over the next slide that explains the Ethics Trace process before pairs start working.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Tracing Bias to Outcome activity guide and one case card.\n\n**Say:** In the video, Dan's team asks who could be harmed. When a model keeps giving one group unfair results, you need a way to work back from the result to the people behind it. The Ethics Trace is five questions, asked in order. You start with who got the unfair result and end with a fix. You will now examine some real cases of bias in real-world scenarios.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the slide to reveal the five steps of the Ethics Trace process.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students should complete the Ethics Trace activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** After a few minutes, check that each pair has finished Spot and Trace steps. If a pair is stuck on Trace, ask: \"Who or what was missing from the training records?\"",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Ethics Trace",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Race comes up in Cases B and D, and gender in Cases A and C. Use the words the cases use, and never ask a student to speak for a group. If a student blames a group of people, ask: \"Which choice led to that?\" The case card key has a link to each real case."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0872ecaa-f79e-4119-9f14-1e6f228a30c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaa30c09-48a8-4339-9aa3-4711b7a9d18e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e81063dd-bc9e-4ca2-92d8-0989c6bda893",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n\n* What choice may have led to the unfair result in your case?\n* What new problem could your fix cause?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should hear that each case involves different choices and different people, and that pairs with the same case may name different people. Listen for a named choice and a named person. If pairs disagree, ask each pair to point to the part of the card that supports its answer. If a pair says the system was just biased, ask: \"Which choice may have led to that?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e81063dd-bc9e-4ca2-92d8-0989c6bda893",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaa30c09-48a8-4339-9aa3-4711b7a9d18e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa4d69d3-9673-4166-b614-12c357960bf1",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students illustrate an unfair result and demonstrate how it can link back to data choices and the people who made them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work on their comic on their own.\n\n**Say:** Tell a story about AI bias in a comic. Use your Lesson 10 app, Playlist Bot from Lesson 6, or your case from today. Show who got unfair results, the data choice behind them, who made it and who else it affects.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 1: students combine four frames, add speech bubbles, write a caption that says why it happened and who could have changed it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for strips that show a data choice and the person who made it. Ask: \"Which frame shows the choice that caused the unfair result?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "AI Bias Comic Strip",
        "progression_name": "AI Bias Comic Strip"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa4d69d3-9673-4166-b614-12c357960bf1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaa30c09-48a8-4339-9aa3-4711b7a9d18e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db732a52-812a-4ba6-be92-2c4b90cf7131",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students share their comics with their peers and reflect on the what they read.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Students will have the opportunity to share their comic. Provide each student with a sticky note or two in order to leave write about something that they notice or something that they wonder about after reading their peers' comics.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Comic Strip Gallery Walk",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short because other activities in the lesson ran long, consider cutting the gallery walk activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db732a52-812a-4ba6-be92-2c4b90cf7131",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaa30c09-48a8-4339-9aa3-4711b7a9d18e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e62c4376-38d1-4a78-9835-808e22b2f5fe",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 2 to reflect on the comic that they created.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Read the Level 2 answers. Look for a specific data choice (who was in the records, a feature, a label or the labeling) and a person who could have changed it. If a caption blames the model or a group of people, ask: \"Which choice led to that?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e62c4376-38d1-4a78-9835-808e22b2f5fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dc63ca37-b7d1-47e2-bb4e-c205e8643a32"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a427cf0-67b3-4211-bd25-a4cee927e116",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Who is responsible when an AI system gives someone an unfair result?\n* *Turn and Talk: partners read each other's captions, then two or three students share theirs with the class.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should see that people can reasonably disagree about who is responsible. Harm can come from choices no one meant to cause harm, and responsibility is often shared by many people. Accept any answer a student supports with a choice from the lesson. If partners disagree, ask each to point to the choice in the caption that supports their answer, and remind them to disagree respectfully.\n\n**Say:** Data choices can hurt people even when no one means harm. You may not agree on who is responsible, and that's OK.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a427cf0-67b3-4211-bd25-a4cee927e116",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dc63ca37-b7d1-47e2-bb4e-c205e8643a32"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48d72c87-a15b-4a18-a438-0eee87bd8035",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48d72c87-a15b-4a18-a438-0eee87bd8035",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dc63ca37-b7d1-47e2-bb4e-c205e8643a32"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3f9a38c-8473-4b56-8d72-733137fcdf05",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students start thinking about what makes a model worth trusting before they build one.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think of an app that picks something for you, like a song or a video. What would you want to know before you trusted its pick?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Most students will start with whether the pick was good. Stay with the ones who ask where the app's picks come from or whether it works for people like them, and ask how they could find that out. Those two ideas are the project."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3f9a38c-8473-4b56-8d72-733137fcdf05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d425c99d-fd7d-496a-8a6f-8707cdc11ece"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "059c5bda-9ce5-43ab-9ba4-bc51970e6451",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day. Tell students they will answer it for a model they build themselves."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "059c5bda-9ce5-43ab-9ba4-bc51970e6451",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d425c99d-fd7d-496a-8a6f-8707cdc11ece"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3fc08ff3-4993-4e9c-890b-444c374961cf",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see the whole project and their role in it before they start.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Become a Data Scientist"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3fc08ff3-4993-4e9c-890b-444c374961cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43f331ca-6969-4f03-8cdf-56b0820ac35c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9faa120f-affd-4334-97b6-6157c64760fe",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this unit, you have practiced the skills that data scientists use in their careers. You choose the data and the label, you train the model, and then you test whether it works for real people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9faa120f-affd-4334-97b6-6157c64760fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43f331ca-6969-4f03-8cdf-56b0820ac35c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe1d8718-9d2e-4680-a277-dc65fe83ef0f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student or group a copy of the Data Makes AI Project guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the Project Overview together. Point out that classmates will test each other's models on the last day, so a model that fails in an interesting way is worth as much as one that works."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe1d8718-9d2e-4680-a277-dc65fe83ef0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43f331ca-6969-4f03-8cdf-56b0820ac35c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62ddb003-9e8e-40ee-8186-485c9a6e2773",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students choose a dataset they care about and learn where it came from.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Level 1 to read about the dataset choices, pick one, explore its data cards in AI Studio, and fill in My Data in the guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Meet the Data",
        "progression_name": "Meet the Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62ddb003-9e8e-40ee-8186-485c9a6e2773",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43f331ca-6969-4f03-8cdf-56b0820ac35c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09192321-f93f-4490-ae0e-d9577765ea8f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Students tend to pick by topic and skip the data cards. Ask them who made the data and how many records it has. If a student is stuck between two datasets, ask which one they could explain to a friend.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Who could be affected if a model trained on your dataset gets a prediction wrong?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Push past \"the user\" to a specific person. A student with the snacks data might name a kid whose snack gets turned away. Once a few people are named, ask what a wrong prediction would cost that person."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09192321-f93f-4490-ae0e-d9577765ea8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43f331ca-6969-4f03-8cdf-56b0820ac35c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fa191b1-d3e2-4678-b7b3-f6e85a16c3c9",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students train their first model and follow one prediction through it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- **Level 2:** Students record their dataset, their label & features, who the model is for, and a predictive rule.\n- **Level 3:** Students train Tree 1, fill in Tree 1 in the guide, make a prediction for their user, and save the tree to the Backpack.\n- **Level 4:** Students add Tree 1 from the Backpack to their Project Map and trace their user's path to the prediction.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Training a Model",
        "progression_name": "Training a Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fa191b1-d3e2-4678-b7b3-f6e85a16c3c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43f331ca-6969-4f03-8cdf-56b0820ac35c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e47123d0-9b8a-418d-b871-969bb630cdfd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students finish Level 2 before they train, since the rule only works as a comparison if it comes first. On Level 4, ask students to say each branch out loud as they follow it.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "key": "tip-12-1-6-0",
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 can be used as a formative assessment. A strong answer names a specific person and says what the model predicts for them. An answer that needs support says the model is for \"anyone\" or describes the goal as getting the model to work."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e47123d0-9b8a-418d-b871-969bb630cdfd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "43f331ca-6969-4f03-8cdf-56b0820ac35c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc8685ab-505a-491d-958a-e839c48c9d40",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Where did your tree surprise you?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students who wrote a rule in Level 2 can compare it to the top of their tree. When a student says the tree checked a feature they didn't expect, ask what in the data might have led to that. Close by returning to the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc8685ab-505a-491d-958a-e839c48c9d40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2c7f3c9-6996-4694-946f-f8656345d35e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "483eb536-cfcd-41a6-962e-058ed4a01fb8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "483eb536-cfcd-41a6-962e-058ed4a01fb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2c7f3c9-6996-4694-946f-f8656345d35e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13d67202-a4ec-4d7c-89b0-57c7bbb7aab4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Yesterday you traced a sample user through your tree. What is one thing about your data you would change to help that user?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Expect vague answers like \"more data\" at first. Ask what kind of data, and for whom. You are listening for three kinds of change that students will meet today: adding a feature, taking examples out, and adding examples."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13d67202-a4ec-4d7c-89b0-57c7bbb7aab4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9937ea48-f726-41f8-b154-715110a822a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1b70c40-bb05-40c7-ab69-6d1c17d6e5a2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1b70c40-bb05-40c7-ab69-6d1c17d6e5a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9937ea48-f726-41f8-b154-715110a822a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55b6c849-22eb-4355-bca4-11e4ce4abbc7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Data Moves Reference.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that each problem on the sheet lists more than one move that might fix it. Students keep the sheet for the rest of the project."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55b6c849-22eb-4355-bca4-11e4ce4abbc7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9937ea48-f726-41f8-b154-715110a822a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e9a416d-ffdd-4563-90aa-99db1afbc90e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students of the data moves from earlier lessons: FEATURE (Lessons 7 and 9), ADD and CUT (Lesson 8) and BALANCE (Lesson 6). More than one move can fix the same problem."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e9a416d-ffdd-4563-90aa-99db1afbc90e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b816f1d6-d804-4134-b069-fa6fb11bb981"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f77dfdf9-2fa0-420e-ae2f-20f67db9ca9d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover that the records a tree gets wrong often share a category that has only a few records.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 1 to train Tree 1 again and fill in Find the Problem in the guide. On Level 2, they complete Prepare Stop 1 in their Project Map and use the Data Moves Reference to pick a move.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Find the Problem",
        "progression_name": "Find the Problem"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f77dfdf9-2fa0-420e-ae2f-20f67db9ca9d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b816f1d6-d804-4134-b069-fa6fb11bb981"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80e2312e-f516-466e-9263-4d1b045395a5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If a student has no Incorrect records, send them to question c: Column Analysis works for every label. When a student picks a move, ask which records made them pick it. None is a fair answer when they can say why.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did you see in your data that made you pick your move?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect their move to evidence in the data, such as a Least Common category or Incorrect records that share a category. Listen for counts. If a student names a move without evidence, ask, \"Which records made you pick it?\""
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80e2312e-f516-466e-9263-4d1b045395a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b816f1d6-d804-4134-b069-fa6fb11bb981"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff2adf0e-daa2-4cf4-b95c-0389a2f48bba",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover whether the move they picked changes their tree, and whether it causes a new problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 and 4.\n- **Level 3:** Students open the (Fixed) dataset for their theme and train Tree 2. They can keep their two features or add more. They fill in Tree 1 vs. Tree 2 in the guide and save Tree 2 to the Backpack.\n- **Level 4:** Students add Tree 2 from the Backpack, place it next to Tree 1, and complete Prepare Stop 2 (What changed? and New Problem).",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Training a Model",
        "progression_name": "Training a Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff2adf0e-daa2-4cf4-b95c-0389a2f48bba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b816f1d6-d804-4134-b069-fa6fb11bb981"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fbf9896-6725-4c25-925b-3c9923a4ab25",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If Tree 2 looks the same as Tree 1, ask whether it uses the feature from Prepare Stop 1. Added records only help when the tree can ask about that feature. Once the two trees are side by side, ask students to point to one place where they differ.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "key": "tip-13-1-4-0",
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 4 can be used as a formative assessment. A strong comparison points to a specific difference between the two trees and connects it to something that changed in the data. A comparison that needs support only says the new tree is \"better\" or \"bigger.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fbf9896-6725-4c25-925b-3c9923a4ab25",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b816f1d6-d804-4134-b069-fa6fb11bb981"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0f792ce-9758-401d-b3c6-57a11e02eaa0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is one thing that changed in your tree?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Every answer should get the same follow-up: why did that change happen? If a student reports a higher score, ask which examples were cut from the data and what the model no longer sees. Return to the Question of the Day and ask who the change helped."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0f792ce-9758-401d-b3c6-57a11e02eaa0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9b4f1764-22c5-4194-9bf3-b18c8653956f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c20f64c5-b437-46ae-9a03-73ea449b2d31",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think of a time an app recommended something that was clearly not for you. Why do you think it got you wrong?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students will have funny examples, so let a few land. Then steer toward causes. The answer worth waiting for is some version of \"it didn't have examples of people like me,\" because that is what they are about to check in their own models."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c20f64c5-b437-46ae-9a03-73ea449b2d31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d97d3418-76ee-4d03-abd1-4dd67545894f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a6ed9cb-48df-4986-bad5-272c417c09df",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a6ed9cb-48df-4986-bad5-272c417c09df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d97d3418-76ee-4d03-abd1-4dd67545894f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b90c457-b864-4601-9610-3992044122d2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students meet the people who might use a model like theirs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student or group the User Profile cards for their dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students read all four cards and choose two to investigate. Tell them the cards don't say which users the model works for. Finding that out is their job.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "User Profiles",
        "progression_name": "User Profiles"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b90c457-b864-4601-9610-3992044122d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcde8d03-1b98-48a0-9c79-d98af9e01da5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a3f815e-a2e7-4757-a58c-f1cdf5f63e62",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test their model for two real-seeming users and trace what happens.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 14, Level 2. For each user, they choose a category for every feature from the details on the card, make a prediction in AI Studio, fill in Test for My User in the guide and save the tree to the Backpack. On Level 3, they complete Try Stops 1 and 2 in their Project Map.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "User Profile Analysis",
        "progression_name": "User Profile Analysis"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a3f815e-a2e7-4757-a58c-f1cdf5f63e62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcde8d03-1b98-48a0-9c79-d98af9e01da5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6399cd9-4f4d-4ff8-8ad7-757ea5a71c7e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who copy every detail from the card. Ask which details their tree uses, and have them cross out the rest. When a prediction looks wrong, ask the student to point to the branch where the user's path split from what they expected.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "key": "tip-14-1-2-0",
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some details won't fit any category in a feature, and some users care about a feature the student's tree doesn't use. Don't solve this for them. A user who can't be traced is a finding, and it belongs in the project."
          },
          {
            "key": "tip-14-1-2-1",
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 3 can be used as a formative assessment. A strong trace follows the user branch by branch and says why the tree ends where it does. A trace that needs support gives only the final prediction, or lists the branches without saying what they show."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6399cd9-4f4d-4ff8-8ad7-757ea5a71c7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcde8d03-1b98-48a0-9c79-d98af9e01da5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c16abb03-92cc-46bb-903e-b6edc967f2cd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students discover who could be affected by their model's mistakes, then turn their test results into a draft model card.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete Try Stop 3 (Ethics Trace: Name, then Zoom Out) and Try Stop 4 (Trust Statement) in their Project Map.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Ethics Trace and Trust Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c16abb03-92cc-46bb-903e-b6edc967f2cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fcde8d03-1b98-48a0-9c79-d98af9e01da5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c3f2aabe-8ed1-4058-9441-88f75f3959be",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which of your two users did your model work better for?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** A name alone isn't an answer, so ask each student what in their tree or their data explains the difference. If several students with the same dataset name the same user, point that out and ask what those models have in common."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c3f2aabe-8ed1-4058-9441-88f75f3959be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f30b730c-337f-4781-b624-73f475c35916"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cbe87121-9b3c-4195-b6a1-f4c88b49f2d5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If a classmate finds a mistake in your model today, is that good news or bad news?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The room will probably split. Ask the \"good news\" side who would have found the mistake if a classmate hadn't. Make sure the class hears that a real user would have, and that is why data scientists test before they share."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cbe87121-9b3c-4195-b6a1-f4c88b49f2d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dbcf2b98-342b-4cd0-aedb-cd9543465786"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d62e9619-fc0e-471b-af62-6bdd9c64cc76",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d62e9619-fc0e-471b-af62-6bdd9c64cc76",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dbcf2b98-342b-4cd0-aedb-cd9543465786"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f3e75a2-ea92-4e33-99fd-f0db2589a7d2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test models they didn't build and give each builder evidence to work with.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student or group a small stack of sticky notes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** First, direct students to Lesson 15, Level 1 to test their model for the new user and fill in New User in the guide. On Level 2, they complete Reflect Stop 1. Then have students open their Project Map to their decision tree and model card, and leave their User Profile cards at their seat. Split the class in half. One half stays to host first, and the other half visits.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "User Testing",
        "progression_name": "User Testing"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f3e75a2-ea92-4e33-99fd-f0db2589a7d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "51d5adf1-b5e7-4001-b894-4f2148f23b14"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c3ba42cb-55b3-4679-bd36-1b2f584eb9c6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Run two 10-minute rounds, and swap hosts and visitors between them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Read sticky notes as they go up. If a note only says the model was right or wrong, send the visitor back to add the reason. Visitors who finish early can test a case of their own, like a snack or a song they chose."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c3ba42cb-55b3-4679-bd36-1b2f584eb9c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "51d5adf1-b5e7-4001-b894-4f2148f23b14"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89fdc593-f8a1-4d58-840b-a3b125190c07",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use what classmates found to finish their model card.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students quiet time to revise.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "key": "tip-15-1-2-0",
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The revised model card can be used as a summative check. A strong model card names who the model works for and who it doesn't, and backs each one with a specific prediction or sticky note. A model card that needs support stays general, such as \"it works for most people,\" or leaves out the feedback."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89fdc593-f8a1-4d58-840b-a3b125190c07",
        "lesson_activity.key": "51d5adf1-b5e7-4001-b894-4f2148f23b14"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "270224d5-a80a-4bb8-9fda-c42c1d4a3a44",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What does a data scientist control when they build a model?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Collect answers until the class has named the data, the label, and the features. Then ask who is responsible when a model gets a user wrong. This is the close of the unit, so let students hear that the choices were theirs and so were the results."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "270224d5-a80a-4bb8-9fda-c42c1d4a3a44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8df7742e-b18c-4ab0-83da-bf96387962be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c46f8f4-294f-4672-8ca7-cb9e835fc23f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Practice with the Unit Vocabulary"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c46f8f4-294f-4672-8ca7-cb9e835fc23f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "11b0f76a-e63c-40ff-a2f4-baf0efa4c1f5"
      }
    }
  ],
